//doi.org/10.2307/1968538":{"authors":"Stone (1932)","title":"On One-Parameter Unitary Groups in Hilbert Space","url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1968538"},"https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006":{"authors":"Aaronson & Ambainis (2014)","title":"The Need for Structure in Quantum Speedups","url":"https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"},"https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.045.026":{"authors":"Lanczos (1950)","title":"An iteration method for the solution of the eigenvalue problem of linear differential and integral operators","url":"https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.045.026"},"https://www.unige.ch/~gander/HistoricalReferences/GALERKIN.pdf":{"authors":"Galerkin (1915)","title":"Rods and plates: series expansions for problems of elastic equilibrium","url":"https://www.unige.ch/~gander/HistoricalReferences/GALERKIN.pdf"}},"groups":[{"scope":"Theorem 12, k=1, M≥2. The counter Born distribution for a unitary preparation has the BHMT amplitude-error guarantee with success at least 8/π². The expanded circuit counts M−1 controlled marking calls plus initialization. The paper counts M evaluations of its function.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Theorem 12 for k=1 and M≥2. The Born law meets the BHMT error window with probability at least 8/π². Probability-one endpoint outcomes hold for a=0 and for a=1 with even M.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Counts the expanded implementation’s M−1 marking calls, gates, preparation accesses and source reflections in its explicit circuit vocabulary. The preparation budget is separate from the marking-query convention.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Theorem 12, M≥2 and all integer windows k≥2. The actual prepared circuit satisfies the k-dependent amplitude error bound with success strictly above 1−1/[2(k−1)].","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"The prepared Born distribution satisfies the strict k≥2 amplitude tail of Theorem 12 for M≥2. The proof handles zero and unit amplitude and otherwise uses the two-eigenphase mixture.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Columnwise Hadamard conjugation changes the phase on the all-ones control and target sector into a phase-free XOR of the target. Updating the target leaves its disjoint control predicate unchanged.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"An exact, clean- and dirty-ancilla-free k-controlled NOT on the given physical register costs at most 1442k+4323 arbitrary one-/two-qubit gates. The word uses a Hadamard on the target on each side of the already compiled phase of angle π. Equality of full matrices eliminates residual global and relative phases.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"Little-endian increment wraps precisely at the all-ones word. Decrement undoes it for every bit width and every bit pattern.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"A list of phase gates addressed from least to most significant control contributes its binary numerical value times the angle step when the target is on. Composition adds the phases in execution order.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"Exact matrix of the phase-gradient and increment/decrement construction, on all basis columns. A separate target phase supplies the constant increment on ordinary inputs; wrapping the control value activates the desired angle. The modular permutations are still named instructions at this intermediate level.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"Each gradient costs one two-qubit phase per control. The full word has 2k+1 one-/two-qubit phases and one forward and one backward modular increment, whose physical cost is proved separately.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"Cuccaro majority/unmajority ripple carry lowered to actual Qatlib legal one-/two-qubit words, counting each Toffoli as 15. The k paired low bits plus outgoing carry cost at most 34k+1 primitive instructions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Disjoint wire addressing allows the unknown addend, carry, and outer wires of the recursive majority ladder to be tracked individually.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184"]},{"scope":"Cuccaro addition with an arbitrary borrowed carry and high target. The low target plus weighted carry-out equals the two input low words plus the initial carry. The high target is XORed with the carry-out and all spectator bits are restored.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184"]},{"scope":"A legal phase-free Boolean gate word has an equal-length inverse. Local CNOTs implement a controlled bitwise complement, and little-endian concatenation and complementation have their ordinary modular arithmetic meanings.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"The compiled ripple adds the preserved garbage addend and incoming carry modulo 2ᵏ. Conjugation by a conditional bitwise complement changes the sign of an increment without changing its magnitude.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Controlled or unconditional addition of one on k target wires using k borrowed wires, restoring their arbitrary contents. In the proved variant the inverse ripple and forward ripple see opposite input carries when enabled, so the unknown addend cancels. Counting actual one-/two-qubit gates gives ≤70k+2.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Paired views of two equal-size disjoint registers yield a controlled increment. Toggling one borrowed selector twice transfers any predicate stable outside the target and selector to that increment, with full restoration.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"A low register and a high register can be joined when the high increment fires on the original low carry. The proof includes the all-ones wrap and the empty-word case.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Gidney §2.10, implemented with a borrowed-carry ripple variant. Equal register halves exchange workspace and an odd width peels its high-order controlled flip. Any k distinct addressed wires can be incremented modulo 2ᵏ with one borrowed wire in ≤720(k+3) legal one-/two-qubit Clifford+T instructions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0410184","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Products of computational-basis permutations with column phases compose on arbitrary states. A verified reversible finite circuit supplies its actual list of legal addressed primitive matrices in the product order.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Gidney Fig. 21 and the phase-gradient method also studied by Huang–Palsberg. For k controls and an arbitrary angle, an exact phase on the marked basis sector needs ≤1442k+4321 one-/two-qubit gates and zero extra qubits. The proved increment uses the target as dirty workspace; arbitrary exact controlled phase rotations are allowed.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.1145/3656436"]},{"scope":"Physical gate words compose and admit wire reindexing. Three ordinary CNOTs give a SWAP and the appropriate diagonal on the two signal flags is unitary for any real angle.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A phase addressed to an arbitrary control and the outer flag is routed with at most seven gates in the existing physical vocabulary. Applied to the entire encoding register and both flags, the circuit implements the cubic amplification coordinate reflection for every system size, including zero system wires.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Source Theorem 1 compilation after replacing the cubic reflection by the ancilla-free synthesis. There is no lower-system-dimension restriction. Binary padding preserves the block and the evolution; each fixed signal-processing instruction contributes at most 11000(a+4) one-/two-qubit gates for a encoding qubits.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1 standard-form simulation for arbitrary finite dimensions, including a one-dimensional system. With a=⌈log₂ d⌉, r=⌈log₂ N⌉, and the least positive Q of Eq. (1), the same oracle-independent word uses a+r+2 wires, 54(64Q+1) controlled preparation/adjoint calls, 27(64Q+1) controlled block/adjoint calls, and ≤11000(a+4)[27(64Q+8)−2]+2 elementary gates. Its compressed coordinate block has error ≤ε²/2 and success ≥1−ε² for 0\u003cε\u003c1. The linked parity lower bound shows the worst-case query count is optimal up to a constant factor.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"],"related":["FQChem.Complexity.BlockSimulationPrecision.worstCaseBlockCutoff","FQChemQatlib.Dynamics.LowChuang.finiteCoordinateSimulation"]},{"scope":"Reversing and taking the adjoints of the addressed gates uncomputes the borrowed-register arithmetic. The forward and inverse words have the stated combined gate count.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07884"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.2.a, Eqs. (D1)–(D3). Each local Bell projector is an amplitude outer product. For every positive trace-one n-qubit density matrix the Born weights of the full product Bell measurement on two copies are nonnegative and sum to one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.2.b, Eqs. (D5)–(D6). The shared Bell measurement record gives a sign in {−1,1} with expectation |Tr(Pρ)|² for every subsequently specified Pauli P.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.2.b, Lemma 1, Eq. (D8). For N>0 independent two-copy Bell rounds on any density operator and each u>0, the chance of a squared-expectation error at least u is at most 2 exp(−Nu²/2).","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.2.b, Eqs. (D9)–(D10). The squared error of the clipped square-root estimator for y≥0 is bounded by the absolute error in estimating y².","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.2.b, Corollary 1, Eq. (D11), the quantum upper half of Theorem 6. For every n-qubit state and each Pauli revealed after collection, 2,048 Bell rounds on 4,096 copies give additive magnitude error at most 1/4 with success probability at least 4/5.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.4, Task 1. Distinct Pauli words are Hilbert–Schmidt orthogonal. For the normalized state (I+aP)/2ⁿ, |a|≤1, the nonidentity query Q has expectation of magnitude |a| if Q=P and zero otherwise.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplement D.4, Task 1, Eq. (D39), quantum upper bound at failure probability 1/5. For every hidden sign and distinct nonidentity pair P,Q, 256 Bell rounds (512 copies) select the larger magnitude in both label orderings using the same record. Theorem 7 concerns the conventional lower bound.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Composition and primitive Clifford+T words preserve the exact basis action and resource bounds. A Toffoli is expanded into fifteen local gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"A toggle through borrowed binary registers has a computable change in its final control predicate. Running the chain in reverse restores every temporary register.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"The borrowed-wire ladder realizes a many-controlled NOT using linear-size local gate words, following Lemma 7.2.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"The balanced split of Barenco et al., Lemma 7.3 and Corollary 7.4, implements a k-controlled NOT with one borrowed qubit and at most 120(k+3) addressed one- and two-qubit gates. The matrix action includes arbitrary superpositions and restores the borrowed wire.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"The two-qubit controlled NOT realizes its classical action on every computational basis ket.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Turturean, Corollary 1.2, proves the Aaronson–Ambainis influence conjecture on the uniform Boolean cube. For a [0,1]-valued polynomial of degree at most d and variance at least ε > 0, some squared-flip influence is at least (ε/d)⁷.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"]},{"scope":"The pinned proof converts Turturean’s Theorem 1.1 to the {0,1} cube and the unnormalized squared flip difference. The bound has coefficient 8√15/(125π⁴) multiplying ε^(5/2)/d⁶.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"]},{"scope":"Applies the proved Boolean influence bound to the acceptance probability of a T-query phase circuit on independent uniform input bits, assuming positive variance at least ε.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006","https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Applies the explicit squared-flip influence bound with degree 2T to a chosen orbital population after T binary onsite pulses, assuming uniform independent inputs and variance at least ε.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006","https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Computes the uniform mean of a function on one Boolean bit for the two-orbital witness.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"]},{"scope":"For the specific one-bit orbital probe, the Bernoulli antibonding readout has variance 1/4 and squared-flip influence 1.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"]},{"scope":"Instantiates the explicit influence inequality for a single binary pulse, the antibonding readout, and variance 1/4.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09773","https://doi.org/10.4086/toc.2014.v010a006"]},{"scope":"Represents a binary phase oracle or coherent bypass by a degree-at-most-one real factor. Multiplying an amplitude by it adds at most one to the degree.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"A fixed complex mixing matrix acts linearly on the real and imaginary polynomial components and preserves the common degree bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Beals et al., Lemma 4.1, specialized to phase queries. After T queries the real and imaginary amplitude polynomials evaluate correctly and have total degree at most T. Boolean multilinear reduction is unnecessary for these bounds.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Relates squared amplitudes to a complex inner product, preserves mass under phase queries and fixed unitaries, and bounds the probability of a finite basis event by [0,1].","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Beals et al., Lemma 4.2, for a T-query phase circuit. The constructed real polynomial agrees with the event probability on Boolean inputs and has total degree at most 2T. Lean uses ordinary polynomials.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"The diagonal one-particle Hamiltonian with entries 0 or π is Hermitian, and its unit-time exponential implements the sign oracle on the addressed orbitals.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Amplitude and probability identities identify alternating orbital mixing and binary onsite evolution with the phase-query circuit in the polynomial method.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"The one-bit interference example uses 1/√2, a normalized bonding state, and a unitary bonding/antibonding readout.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Calculates a two-orbital realization of a single-bit query. The antibonding probability equals the encoded bit.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"The product of Boolean signs equals ±1 and is orthogonal under the cube sum to every polynomial of total degree strictly below the number of bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.81.5442"]},{"scope":"In the library’s two-orbital model, the diagonal onsite energy is bounded by π. Evolving for time N implements a relative parity sign, which determines the antibonding population for N > 0.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.81.5442"]},{"scope":"For individual Boolean potential-term queries, uniform additive error \u003c 1/2 on the parity-encoding population at time N forces N ≤ 2T. Berry et al., Theorem 3, uses sparse matrix row queries and trace distance for a different Hamiltonian family.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.81.5442","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"For the complementary projector Hamiltonian I−P, the kernel is the marked subspace and the normalized energy equals one minus its population.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9701001"]},{"scope":"Relates the marking oracle to a subspace reflection and checks normalization for both the queried trajectory and the query-free reference trajectory.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9701001"]},{"scope":"Bessel’s inequality supplies the address bound for orthonormal candidates. The sum of projection amplitudes for a normalized reference is at most √N.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9701001"]},{"scope":"BBBV hybrid argument for reflection queries to subspaces satisfying the address bound. Summed distance from the reference after T queries is at most 2T√N.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9701001"]},{"scope":"Converts the hybrid estimate into a success-probability or complementary-projector energy bound for uniform performance across marked subspaces. For orthonormal candidates, energy error ε implies N√(1−ε) ≤ (2T+1)√N.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9701001"]},{"scope":"The one-particle-per-pair qubit encoding is legal, and zero occupation defects characterize the code sector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Checks the occupation permutation and phase for each encoded Pauli word: moves preserve particle number and legality, reproduce the logical action on the code, and have bounded phase magnitude.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Splits norm and expectation over legal and leakage sectors, and bounds the magnitude of each phase-weighted permutation expectation by the state mass.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Quantifies possible energy gain off the qubit code by total coefficient strength times leakage mass, and bounds this mass by the pair-occupation penalty.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Restricting the zero-extended encoded state recovers the spin state on the code and zero amplitudes in the leakage sector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"The two-mode encoding in the spin-to-fermion reduction preserves norm and the full energy expectation for the zero-extended encoded spin state.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Checks homogeneity under complex rescaling and extends a normalized Rayleigh lower bound to arbitrary amplitudes, including zero.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"For real Pauli coefficients and J ≥ 0 with J ≥ b + total coefficient strength, the constructed fixed-number Hamiltonian preserves the spin YES and NO energy thresholds for arbitrary normalized amplitudes, including leakage.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Computes creation–annihilation products on the four pair occupations and checks conjugate phases under the reversed encoded Pauli move.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Checks the two-mode hopping representations of Pauli X and Y, the occupation difference for Z, and the projector penalizing zero or double occupation via (n₀+n₁−1)².","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"A k-site Pauli term touches at most 2k modes and the register has 2n modes. For m coefficients bounded in absolute value by B, their total strength is at most mB.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Identifies the expectation of sums of permutation kernels and the Hilbert-space operator with the spin or fixed-particle energy formulas used in the reduction.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"For real coefficients and real J, the finite occupation-space matrix including the pair penalty equals its conjugate transpose.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Rayleigh–Ritz applied to the encoded fermionic operator gives an upper bound on ground energy for each isometrically prepared normalized trial state.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"A normalized trial within ε of the variational optimum, readout error ≤ δ, sufficient pair penalty and ε+2δ \u003c b−a yield a threshold test for the spin energy promise.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503","https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213"]},{"scope":"Matrix identities for one-bit Pauli entries, tensor-product words, dual coordinates and their conjugation or norm bounds support the real-coefficient encoding.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Pauli basis resolution expands an arbitrary finite local table. Hermiticity makes coefficients real and there are exactly 4^k candidate words on k qubits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Extending Pauli words by identities embeds a table on an injective list of wires, preserving its entries and bounding the support by the arity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Expands a sum of m k-local tables exactly, bounds the number of words by m·4^k, and bounds coefficient strength in terms of arity and a uniform bound on the entries.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Composes finite Hermitian table expansion with the dual-rail penalty. Under a sufficient penalty, the normalized energy promises transfer to the n-particle occupation sector with the same cutoffs. Arbitrary k-local inputs may yield higher-body interactions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Finite matrix expectations on normalized states have a nonempty range bounded below, permitting the use of an infimum for ground energy.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"For a padded circuit, the positive integer denominator D defines cutoffs 1/D and 2/D. The implementation bounds D by 2¹³·130·s⁴, where s is gates + qubits + 65.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Explicit resources for the five-local history construction of §14.4. The encoded terms are Hermitian, have norm at most one, and satisfy gate-, clock-, and initialization-dependent count and encoding-length bounds.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"For a legal verifier with T gates, a normalized witness of acceptance at least 1−1/[2¹⁴(T+65)²] gives low energy. Acceptance at most 1/4 for every legal normalized witness gives high energy for every normalized history state. The three declarations express this via states, an infimum, or a bit-string promise.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The Qatlib-derived Hamiltonian and denominator can be recovered uniquely from the chosen bit-string encoding, whose length is polynomial in padded circuit size.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Implementation lemmas identify template-generated local tables with the history construction, count propagation, initialization, output and clock descriptors, and bound scalar table entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Separated cutoffs make the two energy promises disjoint. Ground-energy estimation within one third of the stated gap bound, or normalized preparation and measurement each within one fifth, decides the verifier promise.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Linear circuit evolution commutes with complex rescaling, and the zero ket obeys the verifier initialization predicate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"If every work wire must start at zero, all legal normalized witnesses give the same acceptance as the zero ket. Its high or low acceptance at the specified thresholds suffices for the history-energy bounds.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Checks distinct wire addresses, involutivity of the majority-controlled update and preservation of the three vote bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib’s 15-gate Clifford+T word acts on three distinct wires as a Toffoli on computational-basis inputs. This is the gate used three times in the coherent readout.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Composes three Toffolis to XOR the majority of three distinct vote bits into a fourth wire. The legal 45-gate word implements that permutation on basis kets and on arbitrary amplitudes.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"For a clean answer wire the readout probability is the total mass of the majority patterns. Independent votes with marginal p, in particular three independently prepared copies, give 3p²−2p³. This is the witness-free gadget used for repetition amplification.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The Born weights of independent product amplitudes factor, and three-vote failure obeys the same cubic recursion as success.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"For initial decision error at most 1/3, iteration of the three-vote map obeys the explicit geometric, three-round halving, and doubly exponential bounds proved here.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Computes gate actions after an injective relabeling of wires, preserving the spectator amplitude factor. Cases cover a single-wire matrix and CNOT on distinct wires.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Relabeling a legal gate word on an injected register preserves legality and length. Its evolution on a factored extension acts only on the selected register.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Verifies disjoint wire addresses and factorization of zero initialization and evolution for three independently prepared copies.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"For a legal witness-free gate word on the zero input, moving three copies to disjoint registers and appending majority uses 3T+45 gates and changes acceptance p to 3p²−2p³.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Checks all-zero initialization after a round, the gate and width recurrences, and the unit-interval bound on acceptance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"After k recursive rounds on zero-input circuits, the acceptance and rejection probabilities equal k iterations of the three-vote cubic on the original success or error.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Bounds the size and precision budget for the library’s recursively tripled circuit. At 9+floor(log₂ s) rounds the amplified padded size is at most 4⁹s³.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"With initial decision error in [0,1/3], the chosen majority depth reaches error at most 1/(2¹⁴u²), where the final size u satisfies the proved growth bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Unary arithmetic, index enumeration and integer logarithm compute the amplifier depth. The correctness and step counts use the library’s list-cell cost model.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Checks legality, parsing and cost for finite gate-code words. Decoding an encoded legal circuit reconstructs its gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Renumbers a gate or word onto a disjoint register, letting the compiler emit three copies with bounded list-processing cost.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"One amplifier layer and k iterated layers implement the specified majority circuits. Width and circuit growth control the step bounds.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Relates gate encodings, unary row weights, and tape emission cost to the sizes of the register and gate word.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Output routines serialize the all-zero-input circuit word, bound the emitted length by steps, and bound serialization or parsing time in the chosen representation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The internal register compiler emits the encoded repeated-majority circuit, with a monotone polynomial cost bound and sufficient steps to write its output.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The bit-list compiler maps a serialized decision circuit to its amplified word and has a polynomial step count in input length. Each output bit is covered by that budget.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Parsing a serialized gate row, a local-term descriptor row, or a sequence of such descriptors recovers its input.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Implements counting and padding by 64 gates and agrees with the padded Qatlib gate list, with explicit list-processing costs.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The tape scanner agrees with its loop. Consumed tape length bounds parsing cost and the unary sizes of decoded fields, rows, and their individual entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Unary tally and field emission produce the expected cells. Step bounds account for their size and for the cost of writing the tape.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Connects the parser with emission of tallies, fields and full records. Scanning serialized rows recovers the records.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Tracks whether the step count pays for the output tape, and proves a cubic bound on template compilation for inputs of bounded row weight.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Generates initialization and propagation templates for the unary clock and finite gate list. Checks their contents, write accounting and cost.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Enumerates the clock-pair templates in rows and columns, with bounds on their emitted size and list traversal costs.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"From an encoded finite verifier, the tape compiler emits descriptors decoding to its five-local Hamiltonian and rational cutoffs, and thereby preserves the amplified verifier promise.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The decoded tape cannot satisfy both the YES and NO energy promises because the cutoffs are strictly ordered.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Bounds compiler steps by 100(L+70)³ on L tape symbols and accounts for every output cell. A preceding generator with the stipulated clock, output bound and semantic correctness composes with this compiler.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Implements the tape alphabet by bit pairs. Parsing and tail-recursive loops agree with the specification and commute with the compiled output on encoded inputs.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The compact bit output decodes to the specified Hamiltonian and thresholds. Its cost is bounded in the list-cell model and composes with a source generator satisfying the stated bit, time and output-length hypotheses.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Integer-arithmetic helpers for the dyadic coefficient compiler. They characterize little-endian magnitude, subtraction with borrow, increment, and powers of two with explicit width bounds.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"A restoring square-root loop computes floor(sqrt(2^(2b+1))). The proofs track remainder invariants, bit widths, and the number of examined positions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Produces the signed dyadic numerator for each finite gate-table atom at precision b and bounds magnitude width and work. Rounding uses floor(2^b·a+1/2).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Computes rounded integer entries for the finite gate alphabet and the corresponding propagation terms. Atom extraction and gate entries have explicit quadratic bit-position bounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Reconciles potentially repeated Qatlib wire lists with injective local supports and checks basis indexing and agreement on spectator wires.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Reading the history Hamiltonian’s finite tables as local Hermitian spin terms reproduces individual embedded kernels and their matrix sum.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Bounds local entries by the embedded operator norm; each decoded circuit term acts on at most five qubits with entries of norm at most one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"For this encoding each history qubit corresponds to one electron among two modes. The number of Pauli words, support size and penalty are explicitly bounded, with up to ten modes per history term.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"For the amplified-verifier promise, both energy cutoffs transfer from the five-local history construction to the fixed-number dual-rail Hamiltonian. This is a construction of occupation-space operators with a penalty.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Applies the variational lower bound for the encoded fermionic operator to a normalized occupation-state trial.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"A normalized preparation and energy readout, each accurate to one fifth of the proved gap bound, suffice to distinguish the amplified verifier promise for this constructed fixed-number Hamiltonian.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"A sequence of X gates on distinct selected wires loads a specified basis string from the zero register in at most q gates for q wires.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Matches the acceptance probability of the basis-loaded decision word to that of the history verifier, including the degenerate zero-width fallback.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"For a valid clocked uniform circuit generator, each basis input’s amplified-completeness or soundness promise gives the corresponding fixed-number energy bound with explicit size control.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Establishes size growth, full initialization and denominator bounds for the repeated witness-free verifier, including its cubic amplified-size bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"An all-zero-input decision circuit with acceptance ≥2/3 or ≤1/3 satisfies the particular high-completeness or soundness promise required by the history encoding after the chosen majority rounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Maps the ordinary bounded-error decision-circuit promise to low or high ground energy of the decoded five-local template output.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Composes bounded-error amplification, the history Hamiltonian, and dual-rail encoding for decision circuits. The uniform version takes a valid clocked generator and arbitrary basis input.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Identifies the bit-list transducer output with applying amplification and the history-template compiler in succession.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The serialized bounded-error decision circuit maps to decodable five-local terms and rational cutoffs. The gap is inverse polynomial in original size and the transducer has a polynomial clock in the specified bit-list model.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib’s finite gate parser rejects malformed words. Legal words have unitary gates, zero oracle queries and gate count equal to the list length.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Computes the action and entries of a gate on a selected register wire and proves that the classical update corresponding to a CNOT on distinct wires is involutive.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"For Qatlib’s gate vocabulary the interpreter equals matrix-vector multiplication by the gate-word matrix.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib’s self-delimiting integers let the bit representation uniquely determine its five-local Hamiltonian encoding.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Counts propagation, initialization, output, and ordered clock pairs in Qatlib’s concrete local term constructor.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib verifies a 15-gate Clifford+T realization of the Toffoli transformation on distinct wires, pointwise on basis kets, with exact resource counts. Compare the standard decomposition in Nielsen & Chuang, Fig. 4.9.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Identifies the exact integer obtained by rounding a finite gate coefficient atom to dyadic precision for the discretized history construction.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib sums squared amplitudes over a finite basis event. For a normalized state the probability is ≤1, and arbitrary complex rescaling multiplies it by the scalar’s squared norm.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01397477"]},{"scope":"Multiplication by the matrix of an involution permutes pure-state coordinates accordingly.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667"]},{"scope":"Qatlib’s finite-state primitives expand the complex inner product, normalize each basis ket and give nonnegative event weights from squared amplitudes.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9780511976667","https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01397477"]},{"scope":"Sequential composition is list concatenation in Qatlib’s query-word semantics. A unitary oracle and unitary gate steps preserve normalization.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Qatlib checks an explicit five-local dyadic history encoding and energy bounds for its specified verifier promises. This construction supplies part of the circuit-to-Hamiltonian argument in §14.4.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Qatlib bounds the scale and the positive inverse-polynomial gap for its normalized dyadic circuit Hamiltonian.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The padded history Hamiltonian separates legal high-acceptance witnesses from universal low acceptance. Completeness requires deficit ≤1/[2¹⁴(T_padded+1)²] and soundness ≤1/4, with rational thresholds dependent on the encoding.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The circuit used in Qatlib’s history-state soundness estimate has exactly 64 additional gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Encoding-specific size facts control the length of self-delimiting integers and the exact number of real and imaginary table entries for a local term.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"A word of unitary gates acts in execution order, and concatenating k copies multiplies its gate count by exactly k. These identities implement the composition in the fast-forwarding lemma.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-01637-7"]},{"scope":"A hybrid argument accumulates at most kα state-vector distance on k segments. The one-step error is assumed only for normalized inputs with a clean ancilla, while the simulated intermediate states may leak out of that subspace.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-01637-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Lemma 2 (arXiv v2, Lemma 3). Atia–Aharonov, arXiv v2, Lemma 3. For every time t up to kT, k copies of a word for t/k simulate bounded self-adjoint evolution on clean inputs with error ≤kα and gate count ≤kB. B bounds the shorter circuits in the chosen unitary gate alphabet.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-01637-7"]},{"scope":"The Hermiticity and row sparsity hypotheses in Berry et al., Theorem 3, hold for the weighted paths joined by bit-controlled crossings. Each row has at most two nonzero entries, including at the endpoints.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"If two input strings agree on the bits incident to a given level, their entire Hamiltonian rows there coincide. Thus the crossing data for that row depends on at most two bit values.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Theorem 3’s two-track graph, expressed in coordinates labeled by accumulated input parity, has two identical copies of the bare spin chain. The Lean identities identify every matrix entry of this relabeling.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The parity-path step in the proof of Berry et al., Theorem 3. At every physical time the crossed propagator is exactly the lift of the uncrossed propagator. From the initial zero-track endpoint the far-end amplitude vanishes on the wrong parity track and equals the bare path amplitude on the correct one.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.2. Under the injective-or-hidden-XOR-mask promise, a verified retry process halts almost surely with the correct mask (zero denotes injective). Its expected number of trials is at most two, with at most 2(n+3) expected oracle calls and an explicit classical decoder cost in Qatlib’s model.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.2. Under the injective-or-hidden-XOR-mask promise, a verified retry process halts almost surely with the correct mask (zero denotes injective). Its expected number of trials is at most two, with at most 2(n+3) expected oracle calls and an explicit classical decoder cost in Qatlib’s model.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.3. For n ≥ 1, a randomized adaptive tree with at most 2^(n/4) queries guesses the fair branch of the random oracle with probability at most 1/2 + 2^(−n/2). The branches are a uniform permutation and a uniform two-to-one function invariant under a uniformly sampled XOR mask, including zero as in the printed distribution.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.3. For n ≥ 1, a randomized adaptive tree with at most 2^(n/4) queries guesses the fair branch of the random oracle with probability at most 1/2 + 2^(−n/2). The branches are a uniform permutation and a uniform two-to-one function invariant under a uniformly sampled XOR mask, including zero as in the printed distribution.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.4 in the length-indexed query-tree model. For any countable family of randomized trees with q(n) ≤ 2^(n/4), a single oracle family gives the quantum decision procedure success ≥ 2/3 eventually, while each classical family fails to exceed 2/3 at infinitely many widths. At width n each tree queries only the n-bit function.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.4 in the length-indexed query-tree model. For any countable family of randomized trees with q(n) ≤ 2^(n/4), a single oracle family gives the quantum decision procedure success ≥ 2/3 eventually, while each classical family fails to exceed 2/3 at infinitely many widths. At width n each tree queries only the n-bit function.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Shor, §5, Eqs. (5.12)–(5.13). With clock 2ᵗ ≥ N², reduced d/r with 0 ≤ d \u003c r \u003c N, and |rc−d2ᵗ| ≤ r/2, the bounded scan returns (d,r) using at most N(N−1)/2 comparisons. This implements the unique-fraction step with a scan. The paper uses a continued fraction to obtain polynomial bit cost.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Shor, §5, Eqs. (5.12)–(5.13). With clock 2ᵗ ≥ N², reduced d/r with 0 ≤ d \u003c r \u003c N, and |rc−d2ᵗ| ≤ r/2, the bounded scan returns (d,r) using at most N(N−1)/2 comparisons. This implements the unique-fraction step with a scan. The paper uses a continued fraction to obtain polynomial bit cost.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"A two-readout variant of Shor’s order recovery. For a unit a mod N of order r > 1 and clock M > 2N², the least-common-multiple decoder recovers r from two independent modular-order phase readouts with probability at least (8/π²)²/4. The paper’s single-readout coprimality estimate has a different success bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"A two-readout variant of Shor’s order recovery. For a unit a mod N of order r > 1 and clock M > 2N², the least-common-multiple decoder recovers r from two independent modular-order phase readouts with probability at least (8/π²)²/4. The paper’s single-readout coprimality estimate has a different success bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For composite N ≥ 3, N ≤ 2ᵏ and clock M > 2N², the modeled residue-sampling program returns only nontrivial divisors. Its success after R rounds is at least 1−(1−p)ᴿ for Qatlib’s explicit single-attempt lower bound p. This program uses two independent phase readouts; its statement establishes success and soundness.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For composite N ≥ 3, N ≤ 2ᵏ and clock M > 2N², the modeled residue-sampling program returns only nontrivial divisors. Its success after R rounds is at least 1−(1−p)ᴿ for Qatlib’s explicit single-attempt lower bound p. This program uses two independent phase readouts; its statement establishes success and soundness.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The two opposite cluster exponentials are inverse matrices, for any finite index set. This supplies the cancellation in the coupled-cluster similarity transform.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1727484"]},{"scope":"The exponential of a sum of cluster generators acting on disjoint fragments is the Kronecker product of the fragment exponentials, even for approximate fragment amplitudes.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"For independent fragments and a product reference determinant, the similarity-transformed Hamiltonian is the sum of the individual transformed operators lifted to the composite. The projected coupled-cluster energies add exactly.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"A positive-semidefinite energy lower bound and a saturating product eigenvector certify that independent fragments have additive ground energies, including minimization over entangled states.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"For L identical independent copies, exact ground energy and projected coupled-cluster energy are each L times their fragment value. The cluster amplitudes may be approximate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"Lee et al., discussion at Fig. 3A. For L independent copies of a fixed finite fragment, assume the fragment calculation reaches absolute error δ in at most C δ⁻ᵇ work. Solving at δ = ε/L gives total absolute energy error ≤ ε with at most (C+1)Lᵇ⁺¹ ε⁻ᵇ work, counting one energy addition per copy. The fragment convergence rate is an explicit hypothesis.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"Classical certificate check used after a trial: an optional candidate is accepted exactly when it is an integer d with 1 \u003c d \u003c m and d divides m.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"A checked proper divisor and its exact cofactor multiply to the current input, and both recursive arguments are strictly smaller and at least two.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Independent repetitions of Shor’s factor attempt. A row misses exactly when every checked proposal fails; its probability under the explicit product of one-shot laws is the one-shot miss probability raised to the number of draws.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The recursively produced factors multiply to N on every tape, even after unsuccessful trials. For N ≥ 2 all leaves are at least two, and the binary splitting tree visits exactly twice its leaf count minus one nodes.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Counting multiplicities, the splitting tree visits at most 2 floor(log₂ N) nodes, including the leaves. Allowing a full batch at each visited node bounds total charged shots by 256 floor(log₂ N)(floor(log₂ N)+1)(floor(log₂ N)+3).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Prime leaves and repeated factors are handled by the same recursion. If every candidate composite that could be reached has a successful row, all terminal labels are prime; no primality test is run.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Amplification constants for the recursive factoring reduction. D shots with individual success ≥ 1/D cut miss probability to at most 1/2. Repeating this block floor(log₂ N)+3 times bounds the union of failures over at most N+1 possible moduli by 1/4.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Bounded-error completion of Shor’s Section 5 splitting reduction. For N ≥ 1, each composite m ≤ N has a certified single-shot factor probability ≥ 1/[128(floor(log₂ N)+1)]. The adaptive routine returns a product equal to N always, and a list of primes with multiplicities with probability ≥ 2/3. Its cubic-in-bitlength trial bound holds on every run; exhausted batches leave m unsplit.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Sections 3 and 5 use modular exponentiation. The executable binary run agrees with natural powering modulo N and performs at most two modular multiplications per exponent bit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For the Section 5 classical reduction, every reported divisor is proper, even when the order candidate is incorrect. A positive nonunit below N yields its gcd with N before order finding.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 compares the 2-adic valuations of component orders. An unsuccessful unit projects to an element with the full global order valuation under any residue-ring homomorphism to a modulus larger than two.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"In a finite cyclic group of even size, multiplication by a generator changes the 2-adic valuation of the order. This supplies the local half bound for the Section 5 CRT argument.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Counting injection for the Section 5 CRT proof. Once one coordinate of a k-fold product is fixed, a generator shift that changes labels independently in every other coordinate bounds agreement by a fraction 2^(1−k).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5, before Eq. (5.1). Among uniformly random units modulo every odd N > 1 with k distinct prime factors, an even order whose half-power differs from −1 occurs with probability at least 1−2^(1−k). Lean constructs the prime-power CRT decomposition from N.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5, classical reduction. A good unit and its true order yield a proper divisor using the single minus-sign gcd of the modular half-power.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"With t independent uniformly random units and exact orders, at most a fraction 2^(−(k−1)t) of the sample tuples produce no factor. This augments the single-trial bound before Eq. (5.1).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5, random-base reduction. Uniform sampling from all 1,…,N−1 has failure fraction at most 2^(1−k), since every nonunit gives a factor immediately and the candidate is the exact order on units.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For the optional classical perfect-power step in Section 5, binary search makes exactly one power comparison per root bit and recovers any exact integer root in its initial interval.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The implementation checks candidate exponents in descending order. Its power scan returns only proper divisors, finds every exact power with exponent in its search range, and counts the worst-case number of comparisons.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Implements the Section 5 classical treatment of easy inputs. The routine returns a proper factor of every even N > 2 and every perfect power a^e with a,e ≥ 2.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The Section 5 preprocessing implementation uses at most (L−2)(L+1) power comparisons at positive input width L, with natural subtraction. This is an arithmetic operation count.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"If a composite number above one survives classical preprocessing, it is odd and has at least two distinct prime factors. These are the hypotheses of Shor’s order-to-factoring reduction.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For each exponent e ≤ floor(log₂ N), all candidate powers along the binary root search fit in L·e ≤ L² bits. This certifies polynomial operand widths for the classical preprocessing comparisons.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"An explicit fair-bit implementation of the random positive base draw in Section 5. Every target base has a unique accepted word of length 1+floor(log₂(N−1)) and at least half the words are accepted.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Binary datapath primitives used in the Section 5 factoring reduction. On unsigned words that fit in w bits, one 6w-gate ripple subtraction computes a comparison or a saturating predecessor. Fixed-width wiring and branch selection use the repository’s branching-program model.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Computes the gcd of two unsigned w-bit integers by binary restoring division and the Euclidean remainder recurrence. A public limit of 2w divisions suffices and at most 40(w+1)³ Boolean arithmetic gates are charged.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Implements the factoring reduction’s even-number and perfect-power checks. For a b-bit input it tests small exponents by binary root search, carries exact powers in (b+1)² bits, and returns the same optional divisor as the executable preprocessor within 100(b+1)⁶ Boolean arithmetic gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Implements the Section 5 gcd reduction and checks both children of a proposed split. Word-based modular powering, Euclidean gcd and one restoring division agree with extractFactor and certify. Every successful pair includes the actual quotient.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Converts a fair binary word into the same positive base as the probability proof, and detects words outside the allowed range. A comparison, decrement and increment cost at most 18w Boolean arithmetic gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The decoder returns an order candidate at most the clock size. With a proper positive base and a fitting positive clock, the postprocessing branch returns the certified divisor and cofactor from the same observed clock value within 2000(w+1)⁴ Boolean arithmetic gates. Nonunit bases take the gcd branch directly.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For m ≥ 2, a fair word fitting floor(log₂ m)+1 bits, and a measured record below a clock size fitting twice that width plus two bits, all classical branches of a factor attempt return exactly the verified split and cost at most 10⁶(floor(log₂ m)+2)⁶ Boolean arithmetic gates.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"ForShor’s weighted logical-gate count for the allocated order-finding circuit is bounded uniformly over every modulus and legal coprime base by C(floor(log₂ m)+1)³. A finite maximum absorbs the small inputs left open by the asymptotic circuit theorem.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Lifts a bound on one modulus to the actual recursive divisor tree by summing over the nodes the splitter visits. Binary input widths decrease along subproblems.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"At every tape for input N, the adaptively visited subproblems, including all bases in a padded batch, cost at most C(floor(log₂ N)+1)⁶ logical gates in the pinned ForShor resource model. This is an execution count for the specified circuit family.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The fair base word and actual Fourier clock fit the argument widths required by the bit-level factoring program. Taking the finite maximum of the program’s costs over the physical records preserves the polynomial one-shot bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Connects each branch in the physical law to the bit-level program. On a coprime base it processes the same clock result, a nonunit yields its gcd, an oversized draw is rejected, and successful preprocessing returns the divisor together with its cofactor.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For every recursive tape, with L=floor(log₂ N), the total classical trial datapath cost is at most 768000000(L+2)⁹ Boolean arithmetic gates and at most 768(L+1)⁴ independent fair bits need be drawn for base selection. Both counts pad every visited modulus to a full batch.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Every factor fits in the original binary word and there are at most floor(log₂ m) leaves below a recursive call at m ≥ 2. Even copying the complete output list at each return takes at most 2(floor(log₂ N)+2)³ bit writes under the fixed-width tagged representation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Shor Sections 3–5 at the level of adaptive logical-circuit execution. For every positive N the physical procedure returns a product equal to N on every tape and a prime factorization with probability at least 2/3. Worst-case bounds jointly cover weighted logical gates, binary trial arithmetic, random bits and output copies. Shor’s Section 2 uniform circuit-construction and numerical gate-entry generation costs are separate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Tracing out spectator coordinates produces a positive two-particle density operator of trace one from every physical N-fermion density operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"The set of representable two-particle densities is convex, and tracing out one spectator proves the nesting of these sets with increasing particle number (note 27).","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Relabeling antisymmetric particle coordinates preserves the density when applied on both indices. The last-particle partial trace preserves antisymmetry of the remaining state.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"A two-body observable on any chosen ordered particle pair has expectation equal to its trace against the normalized pair density.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"The attainable energies of a finite N-fermion Hamiltonian that sums two-body terms are exactly the attainable values of the linear energy functional on representable two-particle marginals. This is the variational identity used in the hardness reduction.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"Gershgorin bounds the Hermitian trace norm by dimension squared times the largest entry. Explicit Hermitian two-body observables read the real or imaginary entry.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"For a candidate at trace-norm distance at least β from all representable pair densities, an explicit pair observable distinguishes it from every convex mixture of physical block marginals with signal exceeding β/(4d⁴).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"The expectation discrepancy of a bounded verifier statistic forces a rejection probability lower bound for arbitrary correlated finite measurement outcomes.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.98.110503"]},{"scope":"The XOR of the two-site masks of an edge subset is its odd-degree boundary. Products of spin characters give the boundary character; Walsh orthogonality performs the sum over spin configurations.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1, zero external field and homogeneous unit coupling. The physical Ising partition function is 2ⁿ cosh(β)ᵐ times the total weight tanh(β)^|A| of even subgraphs. Inserting a spin character selects the sector with its specified odd boundary.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The Gibbs spin correlation is bounded above by one, so prescribed-boundary subgraph mass cannot exceed even mass. The negative ferromagnetic quantum mean energy is the sum of the two-site sector ratios; the even sector has positive mass.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Insertion or deletion of a specified edge toggles its two-site boundary. A partition by edge occupancy expresses the two-point sector in terms of the even mass and its occupied-edge mass.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Theorem 12 for the zero-field unit-strength couplings used by Troyer and Wiese. For every β>0 the negative quantum mean energy is the even-subgraph expectation of m x + (x⁻¹−x)|A|, x=tanh β. Its pointwise range lies between m x and m/x.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Products over odd sites introduce a positive fugacity u. Summing over all possible boundaries gives (1+u)ⁿ; sector expansion and positivity give a normalized positive boosted measure.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Theorem 10, μ=0 conditioned from μ₀=1/n. Rejecting boosted configurations with an odd boundary gives the exact even law. The success probability for exact boosted draws is at least 1/3, uniformly over β>0 and finite nonempty site sets.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Flipping an edge is a fixed-point-free involution. The single-edge lazy Metropolis acceptance is half the minimum weight ratio, lies in (0,1/2], and satisfies detailed balance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 2 subgraphs-world chain. Choosing an edge uniformly and lazily toggling it with a Metropolis test gives a stochastic kernel reversible and stationary for the normalized boosted distribution. The empty-edge kernel is the identity.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The importance proposal increases x to max(x,1/(m+1)). The even mass changes by at most a factor three. Every nonempty even subgraph of a genuine two-site model has at least two occupied edges, which controls the small-x correction.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"A variant of Theorem 12’s reweighting for unit positive bonds. Under the positive boosted proposal the denominator lies in [0,1] with mean ≥1/9; the dimensionless numerator is bounded by (m+1)² uniformly in β>0. The ratio of their means is exactly the negative energy divided by m tanh β.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Independent finite draws have product mass and permit a one-coordinate recursion. Expanding the centered sum gives its exact second moment, linear in the batch size.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"For a readout in [0,L], k independent approximate draws at unhalved L¹ error η yield mean-square error ≤2 L²(1/k+η²) about the target expectation. Sampling and distributional bias are both included.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The normalized boosted law realizes the subgraph expectations, and finite Metropolis runs initialized at the empty graph are probability laws. Clamping the empirical denominator below by 1/9 controls the squared error of the ratio.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"For k independent t-step runs at any β>0 on a nonempty unit ferromagnet, relative MSE is bounded by 648(m+1)⁴(1/k+η²), where η bounds the actual finite-time unhalved L¹ distance to stationarity. The small-β importance factors and the finite-chain bias are included.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"A trail toggles only its endpoints; disjoint edge unions add parity boundaries by XOR. A vertex with no remaining incident edge has even remaining degree.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 4 canonical paths. In an arbitrary two-site edge difference, construct edge-disjoint maximal trails, starting at an odd vertex whenever possible. Their concatenation visits every edge once; each prefix has at most two extra odd vertices outside the final odd boundary.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"A symmetric difference adds odd-site parity modulo two. During a canonical prefix the two encoded configurations preserve the sum of occupied edge counts; only the bounded set of extra odd vertices can decrease their combined fugacity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 4 canonical-path weight estimate. For T=I△P and U=F△P, with P a prefix of the symmetric-difference edge ordering, u⁴w(I)w(F)≤w(T)w(U). The positive odd-vertex fugacity is at most one.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The canonical order is chosen using only the symmetric difference of the endpoints, and its pre-edge prefixes stay within the input bond set.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Knowing the pre-transition configuration T, the toggled edge, and U determines I△F=T△U. Its fixed order determines the prefix, then I and F are recovered by undoing its flips.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 4 canonical path encoding. The total endpoint weight using a given current state T and edge is at most w(T)Z/u⁴ by injectivity and the four-power fugacity comparison.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"A physical edge toggle is symmetric difference with its singleton mask. Telescoping with finite Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the squared endpoint difference by the number of available edges times the sum of the squared gradients along the canonical path.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"All endpoint pairs are summed according to their weights and rerouted by current state and edge. The verified congestion bound compares the full pair quadratic form with the sum of edge gradients.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"A single flip changes the edge count by one and exactly two vertex parities, so a homogeneous boosted edge with 0\u003cx,u≤1 is accepted with probability at least x u²/2.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 4 mixing mechanism in Poincaré form. The stationary endpoint-pair mean squared difference times x u⁶ is at most 2m² times the stationary one-step squared difference for the actual lazy transition kernel. This gives an explicit polynomial relaxation coefficient at the boosted parameters.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The boosted finite mass normalizes to a positive law; the mean squared difference for two independent draws is twice the variance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Detailed balance, laziness and the proved Poincaré inequality contract the squared stationary L² norm of a centered observable by at least x u⁶/(4m²) per update. The density of the actual empty-start chain minus one evolves by this operator and stays centered.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Section 4 mixing result for the homogeneous zero-field proposal. The empty initialization has squared centered density norm at most 2ᵐ. The unhalved L¹ distance after t steps is at most the square root of 2ᵐ(1−x u⁶/(4m²))ᵗ.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"At u=1/n and proposal weight y=max(tanh β,1/(m+1)), an explicit block of 4m²(m+1)n⁶ updates halves the norm contraction factor. The supplied burn-in achieves unhalved L¹ error ≤1/[36(m+1)²s] from the empty subgraph.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Jerrum–Sinclair Theorem 12 for homogeneous unit-strength, zero-field ferromagnetic Hamiltonians. The concrete empty-start lazy edge-toggle chain, run independently for the stated burn-in and batch sizes, approximates the physical quantum thermal energy with relative RMS ≤1/s for every β>0. The stated work counts ideal random edge proposals and real-valued acceptance tests.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The exact sign-free quantum mean energy is zero both on an empty graph at any β and at β=0 for every two-site graph, so those cases require no sampling.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"The actual joint distribution of consecutive Markov states is normalized. Conditioning on the first state proves exact first- and second-moment recurrences for the cumulative readout, with no independence assumption on successive samples.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Changing the initial law by unhalved L¹ distance η changes any entire bounded path expectation by at most its range times η. The proved stationary subgraphs-world distribution preserves expectations under the transition operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"Finite expectation scales and preserves pointwise bounds under nonnegative weights. The expected conditional sum of a centered observable stays zero under the stationary trajectory law.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"For the lazy finite box chain with squared L² contraction 1−r, the conditional expected cumulative readout has stationary squared norm at most 4Var(f)/r². The actual stationary sum of k centered observations has variance at most 6kVar(f)/r, including their temporal correlations.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"For one chain whose initial law has unhalved L¹ distance ≤η from stationarity and f in [0,L], the consecutive k-sample mean has squared error about its true stationary mean at most L²[6/(kr)+η].","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"For the importance-weighted numerator and clipped denominator observed along a single boosted chain, the relative squared energy error is at most 324(m+1)⁴[6/(kr)+η], uniformly over homogeneous zero-field unit-coupling systems with positive inverse temperature and at least one bond.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066"]},{"scope":"All-temperature sign-free comparison for the homogeneous, zero-field Hamiltonian in Troyer–Wiese Eq. (9), with a single burn-in and consecutive Jerrum–Sinclair proposals. For 0\u003cε≤1 the actual mean squared error is at most ε² times the squared quantum thermal energy, and the number of ideal edge updates is at most 16m²(m+1)n⁶[m+2+5184(m+1)⁴]/ε². The counted operations use real-valued acceptance probabilities; no finite-bit arithmetic cost is claimed.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"On the actual nearest-neighbour ±1/0 open cubic spin-glass inputs of Eq. (10), there are at most 3N occupied bonds. The checked single-chain sign-free energy estimator has relative RMS error at most ε with at most 764414784 N¹³/ε² ideal edge proposals, for every β>0 and every 0\u003cε≤1. Zero-bond or β=0 instances have exact zero energy.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Integer division counts accepted bit strings for lazy Metropolis. For positive rational proposal and boost, integer weights with a common denominator give the physical acceptance probability to within one unit of the random-word precision.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Two finite uniform words select the proposed bond and its acceptance. Every readout in [0,1] incurs at most (m+1)/2ᵇ one-step error for m bonds, including non-power-of-two bond counts.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Row-law errors control arbitrary bounded readouts of an entire correlated trajectory. For rational weights the two-word sampler gives an explicit bound linear in the trajectory length.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Perturbing the thermal proposal changes the Metropolis acceptance ratio by a controlled amount. A polynomial number of precision bits pays for the change over the full graph.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For any positive temperature parameter β, use a rounded dyadic proposal and two words of (m+2)²+2+r bits per update. For a k-step trajectory and readout in [0,L] the expected readout changes by at most kL/2ʳ compared with the real-temperature chain. The dyadic numerator here is specified mathematically by a ceiling.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"From an encoded positive rational inverse temperature, rational arithmetic computes an upper enclosure of tanh β and the boosted proposal. A k-step trajectory driven by two finite words per update differs from the ideal chain by at most kL/2ʳ for any path observable in [0,L]. The direct branch uses 12(s+2) Taylor terms for s-bit accuracy.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/0222066","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.1.a, Eqs. (C3) and (C6). Completing the square for two translated three-dimensional Gaussians and simplifying the normalization factor.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.1.a, Eqs. (C3), (C6), (C8), (C9). Exact Lebesgue overlap of normalized three-dimensional positive-exponent primitive Gaussians with arbitrary centers and exponents. The unequal-exponent prefactor is expressed as a product of the two normalization factors and the Gaussian volume.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.1.a, Eqs. (C15)–(C16). Multiplication of equal-exponent Gaussian primitives shifts the density to their midpoint and introduces a distance-dependent exponential. Applying the two factors to the six-dimensional 1/‖r−s‖ integral recovers the exchange and three-center reductions as special cases.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.1.a, evaluating Eq. (C14) at coincident centers. Three-dimensional radial integration gives 2π/α for exp(−α‖y‖²)/‖y‖. The fourth moment of one normalized orbital is (α/π)^(3/2).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (C14) and (F7). Exact six-dimensional Coulomb repulsion of a normalized Gaussian with itself is √(4α/π). A relative-coordinate transformation, absolute-integrability proof, and radial integration justify changing the order of integration at the Coulomb singularity.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (C32)–(C33). On nonnegative ω, the rounded hopping profile √(ω² exp(−ω)) equals ω exp(−ω/2), and it tends to zero at infinity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.4, Eqs. (C59), (C62), (C63), and the coefficient-range argument following Eq. (C69). Every strictly negative target in the stated range is reached by a positive real γ with α γ²≥ω₀. A single positive β and one separation per edge match the scaled onsite and hopping coefficients. Finite-precision encoding and control of other physical coefficients are additional steps.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, Eqs. (F1)–(F4). In the k-electron sector, an independent k-set gives a zero-energy spin-polarized occupation. Without such a set every occupation has a doubly occupied site or a neighboring occupied pair and costs at least V, assuming U≥V≥0.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section IV, Eq. (14), and Appendix F after Eq. (F2). The coordinate state of every fixed-size spin-orbital occupation is exactly the exterior product of an orthonormal frame, with equality proved in the exterior-power occupation basis.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, Eqs. (F1)–(F3). The expectation of the diagonal spin-orbital Hamiltonian is the probability-weighted sum of onsite and edge costs, including all spin multiplicities on each edge.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, Eqs. (F1)–(F5). Sector operator-norm error ε\u003cV/2, after a scalar shift, gives strictly separated thresholds shift+ε and shift+V−ε. A YES instance has a normalized single determinant; a NO instance has the lower bound for all normalized many-electron states. This reduction assumes the stated norm control for its input matrix.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix C.1 describes separate groups of primitive centers for the graph edges. This construction uses an integer line embedded in three-space, a distinct block for each unordered partner pair, and one point for each ordered pair.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Explicit graph embedding for an alternative compact-support realization of the Appendix F independent-set reduction. The n² centers lie on an integer line with coordinates below 3Rn²+R. For R≥4, all distinct centers are at least 4 apart, designated edge centers are exactly 4 apart, and every cross-site component pair of different nonadjacent sites is at least R apart.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Compact-support alternative to the Gaussian orbitals of Appendix C.1. Translating a twice continuously differentiable bump and rescaling its squared norm produces unit-mass nonnegative densities supported in unit balls.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For the compact-support alternative to Appendix F, the physical six-dimensional density product divided by distance is absolutely integrable at all centers, including coincident centers. The proof uses local integrability of the three-dimensional Coulomb kernel and a measure-preserving shear.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For normalized C² orbital components in unit balls, their physical direct Coulomb integral is at least 1/(dist(A,B)+2). For centers separated by at least D>2 it is at most 1/(D−2). The lower bound includes the onsite case because the diagonal has zero product volume.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The alternative construction uses a fixed explicit radial C² profile of unit support, bounded between zero and one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section IV A and Appendix F, using an alternative three-dimensional compact-support construction. Each spatial orbital is a sum of n identical translates, normalized by √n. Disjoint supports give an exactly orthonormal frame and a pointwise formula for the density.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II, Eq. (8), and Appendix F. Off-diagonal Coulomb integrals vanish pointwise by disjoint supports. Direct coefficients are nonnegative and equal the average of n² absolutely integrable component interactions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, independent-set reduction. The actual continuum coefficients in this alternative construction satisfy onsite ≥1/(2n²), edge ≥1/(6n²), and nonedge ≤1/(R−2).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, Eqs. (F1)–(F4). The chosen center-spacing parameter is 12n⁴+2. The energy of each normalized occupation superposition in the diagonal fixed-particle matrix is the weighted average of its repulsions plus the common shift.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Appendix F, Eqs. (F1)–(F4), via a constructive alternative compact-support geometry. An independent k-set yields an exterior Slater state whose Coulomb energy is at most 1/(12n²); without one, every normalized k-electron state has Coulomb energy at least 1/(6n²).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II, Eq. (6). For the compact-support alternative, translation commutes with the three-dimensional Laplacian, its support lies in the closed component ball, and each primitive contributes the same finite kinetic integral.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II, Eqs. (3), (6), and Appendix F. The physical −Δ/2 kinetic matrix is a common diagonal scalar for these exactly orthonormal spatial orbitals. Its value is exactly 195/32. In the k-electron sector both thresholds are shifted by 195k/32.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II, Eq. (3), and Section IV, Eq. (14). Increasing pairs enumerate occupied two-mode annihilations exactly once. The occupation signs have squared modulus one, and deleting the same occupied subset preserves equality of sector labels.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II, Eqs. (3), (6), (8), and Appendix F. For the constructed physical orbitals the kinetic and ordered antisymmetrized Coulomb coefficients are diagonal. Summing their signed occupation matrix elements leaves the common kinetic offset, double-occupancy costs and all direct offsite terms.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Alternative physical realization of the reduction underlying Theorem 3, Section IV A and Appendix F. The assembled signed one- and two-body Hamiltonian, with actual three-dimensional orbitals, Laplacian and Coulomb integrals and V=0, has a determinant witness below kT+1/(12n²) for an independent k-set. Every normalized state lies above kT+1/(6n²) in a NO instance. T=195/32 is the fixed one-component kinetic energy. The rational threshold difference is 1/(12n²). The companion exact coefficient evaluator supplies a polynomial Boolean-gate bound. The published complexity-class claim also includes general Slater witness verification and NP-completeness of the source problem.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Newton shell identity used to evaluate the alternative Section IV A compact-support integrals. Pushing planar area through squared radius gives a constant π density. In three axial dimensions the height integral on a sphere of radius r\u003cd equals 2r/d in the unnormalized height coordinate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Exact intercenter coefficient evaluator for the alternative fixed-basis reduction in Appendix F. In three spatial dimensions a compact radial charge of unit mass has potential 1/d outside its unit ball. Applying the shell identity twice evaluates the actual six-dimensional Coulomb interaction of two component translates as 1/dist(A,B) whenever their centers are more than two units apart.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Explicit compact-support alternative to the Appendix F orbitals. The radial wave function is (1−r²)^(5/2) for r\u003c1 and zero elsewhere. It is C² across the unit sphere; its squared density and the product with its three-dimensional Laplacian are polynomials inside the ball.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Three-dimensional radial integration evaluates the squared orbital mass as 4π·256/9009 and the unnormalized kinetic integral as that mass times 195/32. A rational polynomial gives the cumulative radial charge for smaller radii.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Alternative continuum construction for Theorem 3, Section IV A and Appendix F. Exchange symmetry allows integration over the region in which one electron has smaller radius. The equal-radius set has product volume zero. Applying the checked shell identity at the larger radius leaves a single radial polynomial integral.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Exact six-dimensional self Coulomb repulsion of the alternative normalized compact orbital equals the rational 396396/185725. This value uses the actual positive-part (1−r²)^(5/2) orbital and the three-dimensional 1/|r−s| interaction.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The finite Boolean-table evaluator produces the same integer component centers as the geometric proof, and translating both arguments of the six-dimensional self integral preserves it.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section II electronic integrals and the alternative Appendix F construction. An executable rational algorithm takes the Boolean graph adjacency table and spacing. It returns every four-index Coulomb coefficient exactly using integer coordinate reciprocals and the known rational self energy, adding n² terms for a nonzero coefficient.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Boolean arithmetic behind the alternative Section IV A reduction. Unreduced nonnegative rational addition uses three shift-and-add multipliers and one ripple-carry adder. A width of (m+1)(b+1) bits handles the accumulated sum of m input fractions whose numerator and positive denominator each fit b bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The finite coefficient circuit reads the two appropriate adjacency input bits, selects the corresponding polynomial-size integer centers, subtracts via two ripple-carry chains and chooses the absolute difference. The n² component pairs include the universal rational self energy when centers coincide.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Polynomial-size Boolean coefficient circuits for the physical compact-orbital construction underlying Theorem 3. The circuit takes the graph adjacency bits for fixed orbital indices and returns two nonnegative integers, a numerator and a positive denominator, representing the exact four-index continuum Coulomb integral. For b=size(396397+3Rn²+R+n²) and w=(n²+2)(b+1) it uses at most n²(24b+30w²+6w)+10w² gates. The reduction uses R=12n⁴+2. The count covers input-dependent selections, subtractions, products, additions and final normalization; indices and size-dependent integer constants specify the circuit.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Section 5 Eqs. (5.2)–(5.4). The explicit joint state has amplitude 1/√Q on each of the Q numerically distinct clock labels paired with its modular power. Its squared norm is one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 uses the modular-power orbit. The natural modular data label depends only on t mod r; distinct residues below the exact order give distinct data basis labels.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 Eqs. (5.4)–(5.7). Multiplying the normalized inverse Fourier matrix by the modular-power state and summing over every data label gives the sum of squared period-class amplitudes. No register-outcome probability formula is assumed.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Sections 3 and 5. The primitive Q-th root gives orthogonal Fourier columns, and the explicitly normalized inverse Fourier matrix satisfies FᴴF=I for every positive Q.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Matrix trace and inverse Fourier unitarity imply that the actual clock marginal of the modular-power superposition has total probability one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 after Eq. (5.13). ForShor’s good-outcome recovery statement transfers to the integer implementation of the bounded continued-fraction scan.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 Fourier order recovery. The ideal matrix experiment and executable decoder yield the true order r with probability at least (4/π²)φ(r)/r. The proof covers every integer Q>r² and specializes to the power-of-two clock N²≤Q\u003c2N² for every valid modulus, including powers of two.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Section 5 selects a binary clock in the half-open interval N²≤Q\u003c2N². The construction and its inequalities apply even when N² is already a power of two.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1, bounded self-adjoint Hamiltonians on a complex Hilbert space. For a smooth finite-rank spectral projection, Schrödinger evolution of any normalized initial vector in its range has distance tending to zero uniformly over scaled time in [0,1]. The proof works for a continuous Hamiltonian path and a continuously differentiable projection, without a spectral gap or a universal rate.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Uniform leakage in the equivalent norm-of-the-orthogonal-residual formulation, with the finite spectral projection specified by its eigenbasis.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Finite rank and inclusion of every selected eigenspace in full place the projection tangent in the norm closure of commutators with the Hamiltonian. The pointwise proof uses the orthogonal complement of the kernel and finite sums of rank-one operators.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Finite spectral projection identities, a rank-one commutator formula and norm-closure arguments used to construct approximate solutions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"An approximate commutator on one interval gives error bounded by its residual times the interval length plus an endpoint correction. A finite partition gives a uniform adiabatic limit from pointwise approximate solutions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Appendix D.2.b, Eqs. (D2)–(D4). Each one-qubit Pauli tensor its own copy is diagonal in the Bell basis. The identity reconstructs its matrix entries by summing Bell projectors times their measured signs, including the sign for Y⊗Y.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"The algebra underlying Appendix D.2.b, Eq. (D6), extends to any complex matrix ρ. Contracting the Bell readouts against ρ⊗ρ gives Tr(ρP)². This extension uses a complex square.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Appendix D.2.b, Eq. (D6). For Hermitian ρ and any Pauli word P, the product of local Bell signs on two copies has Born-weighted sum |Tr(ρP)|². For a density operator this is the expectation under the Bell measurement. The measurement is independent of P.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"For each local X, Y or Z measurement, adding its two eigenprojectors gives the identity. Weighting them by their signs recovers the measured Pauli operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"For a fixed choice of local axes and an arbitrary matrix on the k-qubit support, finite summation factors a product statistic over outcomes into local signed-projector sums inside the Born trace.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"For a weight-k Pauli string on its support, the single-shot local Pauli shadow equals zero unless all k axes match. Multiplying the matched outcome sign by 3ᵏ gives Born mean Tr(ρP). The trace identity extends linearly to arbitrary complex matrices.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Lemma 3, Eq. (S50). For every trace-one matrix on the k-qubit support, the single-shot Pauli estimator has second moment exactly 3ᵏ. On reduced density matrices this is the state-independent moment underlying the paper’s squared shadow norm.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The single-shot Pauli readout is real because it is a real scale times a product of measured signs. This relates its real-valued random variable to the Born trace identities.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"For N independent uniformly chosen local Pauli shots with the Born marginal on a weight-k support, Chebyshev’s inequality gives probability at most 3ᵏ/(Nε²) that the block mean misses its target by ε or more. The proof uses unbiasedness and the exact second moment in Supplementary Lemma 3.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The median is the middle order statistic. If an odd sample of 2r+1 values has median error at least ε, at least r+1 distinct values also have error at least ε.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"With mutually independent block estimates, each failing with probability at most 1/8, a union bound over subsets of r+1 bad blocks bounds the median’s failure probability by 1/(4·2ʳ).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1 for finite families of Pauli words and uniform local Pauli measurements. Reusing N shots per block across M targets of possibly different supports, Nε²≥8·3ᵏ for every target of weight k gives simultaneous failure probability at most M/(4·2ʳ) with 2r+1 independent blocks.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1 specialized to local Pauli shadows, with a different explicit constant choice. For δ,ε>0 use 2r+1 blocks where r is the ceiling binary logarithm of ⌈M/(4δ)⌉. The Pauli Born marginals, independent draws and Nε²≥8·3ᵏ on each target imply simultaneous failure probability at most δ.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"For a bijective finite unitary-basis measurement channel in positive finite dimension and any Hermitian A, the squared shadow norm of Eq. (S7) is attained as the largest Born-record second moment over density states. Qatlib proves this through the positive moment effect.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1 for arbitrary finite families of Hermitian observables and finite, invertible unitary-basis measurement ensembles. Qatlib constructs the Born record law and inverse snapshots. With K independent blocks of L shared shots and Lε² at least four times every centered squared shadow norm, the simultaneous failure probability is at most M·3^⌊K/2⌋/2ᴷ.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The finite unitary ensemble version of Eq. (S7). In positive dimension, the positive moment effect attains the maximal second moment over density operators for any Hermitian observable.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1 for arbitrary Hermitian observables and finite unitary ensembles. The independent Born samples and inverse snapshots are explicit. A per-observable centered squared shadow norm at most Lε²/4 yields simultaneous failure probability at most M·3^⌊K/2⌋/2ᴷ.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The positive-integer minimum in Eq. (1) exists for every real time and positive tolerance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Factorial and geometric inequalities convert any qualifying positive cutoff Q at tolerance at most one into a degree 16Q Taylor truncation with remainder at most ε²/6750. These constants serve the cubic amplification proof.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The local construction encodes the sine and cosine Taylor polynomials with common attenuation sin(π/54). Repeated cubic amplification preserves the two signal flags, multiplies the ideal angle by three and increases the compression error by at most 15 per level.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For a Hermitian contraction given by a same-frame projected unitary encoding, the finite two-flag circuit makes 27(64Q+1) controlled encoding queries at the least Eq. (1) cutoff. It attains spectral compression error ε²/2, clean state-vector error ε and postselection probability at least 1−ε². The fixed-gate ledger counts matrix instructions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The local word calculus tracks products, inverses, oracle-free gates and substitution. A separate counter records each physical query label. These are implementation lemmas for the compiled circuit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Controlling a unitary compute–act–uncompute sandwich needs only the middle operation controlled. The computation and its inverse act on the original register, with both signal flags untouched.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The fixed instructions in the compiled signal polynomial and its amplified word are conjugate-paired fiber phases, flag operations, cubic phases ±i about the signal frame, or adjoints of those instructions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For every finite standard-form input H=⟨0|G†UG|0⟩ at 0\u003cε\u003c1, a single oracle-independent word attains spectral error ε²/2 and success probability at least 1−ε². Its two signal flags suffice. The exact ledgers are 54(64Q+1) ordinary G queries and 27(64Q+1) controlled U queries including inverses. The fixed-gate count is at the level of matrix instructions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Finite-register calculations for Lemma 6 verify the clean coordinate frame, uniform slot amplitudes, reversible permutations, norm-bounded decoder rotations and the encoded matrix entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The row-slot permutation, XOR lookup, public decoder rotation and swap give a projected unitary encoding of the normalized sparse Hermitian matrix. Its controlled implementation makes exactly two ordinary row-position queries and two ordinary binary-value queries.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Lemma 6 for exact finite binary encodings. For any positive entry scale b, the two sparse oracles of Eq. (11) yield a clean-coordinate encoding of H/(db) using two queries to each oracle. A row permutation extends each position map beyond its d designated slots. Taking b to be the maximum entry norm gives the published normalization for every nonzero input.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For an exact m-bit normalized entry code, a fixed decoder and d row slots, the simulation at physical time t uses 54(64Q+1) ordinary calls to each of the position and value oracles, with Q evaluated at tdb. Its compressed operator error is at most ε²/2 and its Born success probability is at least 1−ε². The theorem counts oracle calls.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The two averaging flags can be prepared by single-qubit Hadamards up to a common unit phase. The designated coordinate projector and the unitary change of frame are explicit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A conjugate-paired fiber phase is an ancilla test, a two-flag phase and the inverse test. The two cubic phases are globally phased coordinate reflections conjugated by Hadamards.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"These coordinate identities realize the fixed unitaries on the two signal flags. A CNOT has a single negative eigenvector, allowing a fixed two-flag basis change to produce the selective sign in cubic amplification.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The emitted, addressed Clifford+T words perform the exact ancilla-coordinate toggles on every basis input. The single-flag test borrows the other flag, and the two-flag test borrows one physical system wire. Reversible bit complements select the desired ancilla coordinate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"These word-construction lemmas compile the fixed operators into legal one- or two-wire gates, compose and invert the expansions and preserve the per-oracle ledger and execution semantics through substitution.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1 specialized to an a-qubit ancilla and an r-qubit system with r ≥ 1. The uniform simulation uses a+r+2 wires, 54(64Q+1) preparation-oracle calls, 27(64Q+1) controlled block queries and at most 250(a+4)(27(64Q+8)−2) additional one- or two-qubit gates. Spectral error is at most ε²/2 and success is at least 1−ε² at the least Eq. (1) cutoff.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Binary coordinate inclusion preserves inner products. Extending a matrix by identity on unused basis coordinates preserves unitarity and Hermiticity, respects products, and intertwines the matrix exponential. Padding the preparation and block oracles produces the padded logical Hamiltonian.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For any d-dimensional ancilla and any finite logical system of dimension N ≥ 2, including non-powers of two, the padded physical oracle simulation uses ⌈log₂ d⌉ + ⌈log₂ N⌉ + 2 qubits and a counted one- and two-wire gate list. Spectral error and success are proved for the original encoded inputs.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The computational coordinate frame is isometric. Conjugating the word by the two-flag Hadamard changes its preparation and postselection basis while preserving the compressed matrix and every Born success probability.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1 for any finite d ≥ 1 and N ≥ 2. The least Eq. (1) cutoff controls the preparation and block query counts. There are at most 250(⌈log₂ d⌉+4)(27(64Q+8)−2)+2 individually verified one- and two-qubit gates, including flag preparation and computational-basis readout, on the exact ceiling-log register widths. Spectral error is at most ε²/2 and success at least 1−ε².","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Three Toffoli gates and two single-qubit flips implement one full-adder carry for a strict comparison. Fresh wire addresses keep the inputs and other carries intact.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A most-significant-first pair of p-bit quantum inputs can be compared by a reversible circuit of at most 47p addressed one- and two-qubit gates. A clean seed and clean carries yield the numerical strict less-than bit. The emitted word also has an exact action on initially dirty carries.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The mean of the ±1 less-than phase over a uniform p-bit word is the offset-binary fixed-point input value. The forward reversible threshold comparator, a one-wire phase and the inverse comparator realize this phase coherently.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Replacing selected wire values gives a bijection to a Boolean register while keeping all other wires fixed. A p-gate addressed Hadamard word gives the exact uniform mean of a diagonal phase in a clean diagonal matrix element.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The sparse-entry decoder routes its two numerical comparators across distinct data, carry, seed and component-selector wires. The comparators preserve the real/imaginary selector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"These implementation lemmas check unitarity and primitive-gate counts, preserve the untouched threshold and carry inputs under the random-bit preparation, and establish the selected comparison phase for both Cartesian components.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"One fixed unitary reads two p-bit offset-binary Cartesian coordinates and encodes (x+iy)/2 exactly as its diagonal amplitude with workspace initialized to zero. The circuit uses at most 190p+4 addressed one- or two-qubit gates on 5p+3 wires. This is the binary-value decoder layer for the sparse simulation proof.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Unitary operator norms and compression by isometries bound the norm of the projected signal.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Embedding each input vector at a fixed computational ancilla value preserves its norm.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A finite normalized nonnegative amplitude table extends to a unitary with that table as its designated column. This is an existence and matrix identity used for uniform slot preparation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A selector made of unitary diagonal blocks is unitary.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The block-encoding compression error supplies a squared-amplitude lower bound on successful postselection of a normalized input.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The range of a coordinate-fiber isometry is exactly its computational-basis projector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Cubic coherent amplification is implemented by an expanded query word with an explicit compressed-matrix identity and resource ledger.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Cubic signal amplification triples the ideal angle, obeys a perturbation estimate, and converts compressed operator approximation to a clean-frame vector guarantee.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Parallel direct sums of oracle matrices preserve products, identity, adjoints and unitarity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The addressed primitive codes denote unitary operations when their wire indices pass the legality test.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"An injective endomorphism of a finite basis gives a unitary permutation matrix.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Adding a Boolean control preserves products, identity, adjoints and unitarity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Evaluating a word of legal addressed Clifford+T gates gives a unitary matrix on the complete register.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Evaluating a well-formed query circuit on a unitary input gives a unitary operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Compiling the two sine/cosine phase lists yields the intended Hamiltonian circuit, with checked well-formedness and instruction counts.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Taylor exponential truncations on a bounded interval have an explicit nonnegative remainder bound that eventually falls below any positive tolerance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Hermitian projected encodings implement the real polynomial response, accept a scalar phase and combine the sine/cosine Taylor polynomials to form the complex exponential truncation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Averaging two compatible block encodings preserves isometric frames and yields their mean as the encoded operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Phase sequences exist for real polynomials of the required parity and degree strictly bounded by one on the signal interval.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Finite Taylor sine and cosine polynomials obey degree, parity and uniform magnitude estimates on the signal interval.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A scalar polynomial approximation over the spectrum gives the corresponding Hermitian operator-norm error bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A single-qubit Hadamard sends the coordinate fiber to the averaged frame up to a verified unit scalar.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A most-significant-first binary word represents an integer below 2 to its length. Lexicographic comparison computes strict numerical order for equal widths.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"An addressed Hadamard word on zero bits has the exact uniform column on those coordinates. Counting words below a threshold gives the threshold as a complex-valued sum.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Unitary Hamiltonian evolution preserves normalization. Hadamard and Pauli X are unitary, and involutive basis permutations are unitary and self-adjoint.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Tensor gates multiply componentwise, preserve identities and act unitarily for unitary factors.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For any successful block C within δ\u003c1 of a unitary U in operator norm, a normalized input has unnormalized amplitude error ≤δ. The normalized successful pure state has Euclidean error and actual density-matrix trace distance at most 2δ.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Renormalizing a nonzero vector within δ\u003c1 of a unit vector costs at most a factor of two in Euclidean error.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A matrix whose every entry has norm zero, or whose every row contains zero nonzeros, is zero. The empty sparse-query program implements the exact evolution at all times.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"For an external Hermitian target, a Hermitian table rounding error with at most d affected entries per row and maximum complex-entry error η contributes at most |t|dη to the final spectral error, in addition to simulation error for the stored matrix.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A controlled sparse block uses two ordinary in-place position queries and two ordinary binary XOR queries. Reversible lookup and its inverse run with both signal flags untouched.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Circuit bookkeeping for substitution into the standard-form simulation. Grouping coordinates preserves products, adjoints, identities and evaluation. Replacing the preparation oracle by identity and the block oracle by its sparse circuit preserves forward and inverse execution, counts and membership in the elementary gate vocabulary.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A standard-form block with a reversible value table and a uniform public decoder only reads the diagonal amplitude of that decoder on the clean loaded word. The block matrix element is the product of its two slot amplitudes and this decoder amplitude.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Matrix-column identities and unitarity for the prepared sparse block, the position permutation, the register exchange and the reversible entry lookup. These are local ingredients of the sparse-access construction.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Every Hermitian input with nonzero positions covered by k address bits and exact centered Cartesian p-bit entries has an explicit block encoding scaled by 2·2^k. The decoder is a single input-independent circuit with at most 190p+4 gates.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Padded slots use Hadamards on k selected wires. The binary label lies in the resulting support, the loaded zero word contains the two data components, and d≤2^ceil(log₂ d)\u003c2d for every positive d.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"For the coherent outer control required by QSP, an arbitrary q-instruction binary decoder circuit expands to at most 415440q one- and two-qubit gates on the addressed data and signal registers. The bound is independent of all input entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"On the fixed physical register, each controlled CNOT and each controlled two-bit diagonal can be placed on at most three specified bits. Three-wire exact synthesis gives a constant number of elementary gates; repeated phase indices require only two bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Gate-word certificates for placing, composing and taking adjoints of the sparse decoder instructions and for embedding each of its legal gates. These lemmas implement the local resource account.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Factorial block inequalities prove that rescaling simulation time by a constant changes the least factorial cutoff by at most a constant in the joint time-and-precision regime. A fourfold dilation costs at most nine times the original cutoff.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Regrouping the slot and binary-data registers into the QSP ancilla preserves unitarity, ordinary controlled access and the clean compressed block. Rescaling Hamiltonian and time by reciprocal factors preserves evolution.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The actual controlled sparse block uses exactly two position and two entry queries with at most 2k+415440(n+q) additional elementary gates for a q-gate decoder. Its fixed circuit is chosen before the row and value tables.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Expands the k addressed slot Hadamards and fixed circuits, carries the exact product through the wire packing, and counts the resulting physical instructions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Explicit diagonal and permutation formulas for operations on selected wires. The two signal flags, the row, the column and every data bit have distinct physical addresses. Untouched wires retain their bit values.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"For an n≥1 qubit Hermitian matrix with d≤2^n designated slots and p-bit centered Cartesian components in known units b≤2c, the least factorial cutoff at |t|dc gives an explicit O(Q) bound on each ordinary query count and O((n+p+1)Q) additional one- or two-qubit gates. The same circuit attains spectral error ≤ε²/2 and success ≥1−ε².","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Converts ceiling-logarithmic slot padding and the known entry scale into the unpadded physical-time budget, and bounds the physical word length in n, p and the least cutoff.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"One sparse QSP circuit jointly realizes all resource and accuracy guarantees at padded scaled time 2·2^k·b·t, with k address bits, a nonempty system, and p-bit Cartesian entries supplied by ordinary value and position oracles.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Confirms that physical sparse-oracle substitution in the QSP circuit preserves the elementary-gate classification, the clean preparation and the simulated operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A controlled exchange of two n-bit address registers factors into n Fredkin gates. The proof traces every control, data and spectator wire and compiles each three-wire factor via exact elementary synthesis.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Every unitary on three specified bits admits an exact decomposition with at most 415440 elementary gates, acting identically on all other bit values. The proof applies the pinned Barenco-style Gray-code synthesis bound at n=3.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Explicit restriction to selected physical bits and identity on complementary wires respects products, identity and adjoints. Lifting an exact word preserves its gate count.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"In dimension one a uniform circuit performs two ordinary XOR entry lookups, zero position calls and p+1 one-qubit phases. It restores all workspace and multiplies every complex input amplitude by exp(−ith) exactly for the encoded real scalar h.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Binary phase words multiply to the phase of the represented integer. Phase gates are unitary and reversible lookup conjugates their diagonal action into the clean scalar signal.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Theorem 3 upper resource and success guarantees for a nonempty qubit register with ordinary in-place position and XOR value oracles, any 1≤d≤2^n, and exact centered p-bit Cartesian coordinates in public units b≤2c. Setting c to the supplied positive maximum entry norm gives the unpadded |t|dc cutoff. All workspace starts in computational zero. The two gates to prepare and read the signal flags are included.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"],"related":["FQChem.Complexity.SparseSimulationPrecision.worstCaseCutoff","FQChemQatlib.Dynamics.LowChuang.SparseScalar.scalarEvolution","FQChemQatlib.Dynamics.LowChuang.SparseAssembly.successfulOutput","FQChemQatlib.Dynamics.LowChuang.SparseAssembly.zeroAccess","FQChemQatlib.Dynamics.LowChuang.SparseAssembly.roundedSparseEvolution"]},{"scope":"A power of two strictly above a positive supplied maximum lies within twice that maximum. Every signed lattice value x/2^p with −2^p≤x\u003c2^p has exactly the binary representation used by the physical decoder.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A supplied zero maximum entry norm gives a zero Hamiltonian and exact identity evolution for every simulation time. The empty circuit has zero queries and zero additional gates.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Pinned exact Gray-code synthesis of any k≥1 qubit unitary by single-qubit operations and CNOT gates, with no oracle calls and an explicit bound on word length. The sparse construction applies the bound for exactly three qubits.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"Pinned local-gate semantics. The T and inverse T gates are unitary, and the compiled reflection word applies its matrix product to every input state.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.3457"]},{"scope":"The pinned Hermitian evolution stability inequality bounds the spectral perturbation by absolute time times Hamiltonian operator error. It supplies the last step of the source-rounding estimate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The pinned pure-state trace-distance inequality relates half trace norm to the real inner product of two normalized vectors. It converts the successful-output vector estimate into trace distance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"An arbitrary rectangular matrix acting on a Euclidean vector enlarges its norm by at most the operator norm. It specializes spectral simulation error to each normalized input.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The most-significant-bit-first thermal binary numeral agrees with the finite qubit basis enumeration, giving exact representability on the signed lattice.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"An invertible reindexing of the finite basis preserves Euclidean operator norm. It transports the row/column inequality to the actual qubit register.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The Euclidean inner product of two state vectors agrees with their finite coordinate inner product.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The raw normalized-state predicate is equivalent to Euclidean norm one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"A diagonal matrix with unit-modulus entries is unitary. This verifies the explicit scalar binary phase circuit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The pinned complex-matrix row/column sum bound gives a spectral norm estimate via Cauchy–Schwarz. Applied to Hermitian sparse rounding errors, both sums are bounded by dη.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Uniqueness of the reduced inverse identifies the residue (1 + N − v) mod N with the reference circuit’s Step-5 cleanup constant selected by bounded search.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Squaring a reduced multiplier and its inverse together emits exactly the reference modular-exponentiation stages. Their integer constants require at most 162(w+1)² Boolean gates per exponent control.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Shor §3. From the input modulus and a coprime base, Euclidean inversion and repeated modular squaring produce every controlled multiplier and cleanup residue for the reference clock. The emitted modular-exponentiation gate tree is equal to ForShor’s specification, with at most 624(w+1)³ Boolean gates for generating the integer constants at modulus width w. Gate lowering and finite-precision evaluation of phase entries require separate bounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Classical arithmetic for the modular multiplication constants in Shor §3. For residues u,p below a positive w-bit modulus N, a padded sum, subtraction, and restoring division compute (u+N−p) mod N using at most 22(w+1)² Boolean gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Shor §3 uses the classical modular inverse to implement reversible multiplication. The Euclidean loop maintains a coefficient residue for each remainder and returns a coefficient of the gcd. With valid input residues and t steps covering the Euclidean divisions, the loop uses at most 150t(w+1)² Boolean gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Shor §3, classical calculation of c⁻¹ mod n in the reversible cleanup pseudocode (preprint p. 12). For 1\u003cN\u003c2ʷ and 0≤a\u003cN coprime to N, the executable bounded Euclidean algorithm produces b\u003cN satisfying ab mod N=1 in at most 300(w+1)³ Boolean data-path gates. The explicit bound applies to this circuit-generation subroutine.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Positive damping gives a unique finite-basis response vector at every real frequency, including resonance. The residual norm divided by the damping bounds the error in the vector.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03406"]},{"scope":"The doubled system in Eq. (12) is Hermitian. A vector supported on the second block solves it exactly when its nonzero component solves the original response equation.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03406","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Reciprocal eigenvalue weighting solves the damped response equation in the supplied energy eigenbasis.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03406","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Summing the two frequency branches gives the excited-state sum for dipole polarizability in Eq. (3). The permanent ground-state dipole cancels.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03406"]},{"scope":"Elimination backend used for Lemma 2. Padding with a zero preserves the old factor entries. Materialized tables preserve values, and the deterministic first nonzero pivot scan detects exactly the nonzero rows.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2 uses diagonalization to split the two-site tensor. This implementation instead constructs an exact rectangular rank factorization by pivot elimination over a field with exact zero tests.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Eliminating a nonzero pivot strictly reduces the row-space dimension. The number of returned rank-one components equals the input rank, including for singular and zero matrices.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"For an r by c dense matrix, pivot scans and the cached residual updates charge at most rc+r²+2r²c field operations and equality tests. This supplies the cubic refactorization cost used in the two-site update.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 1, Eq. (16). Replacing the addressed site by the one-qubit matrix applied to its physical index implements the gate for arbitrary left and right environments.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2, Eq. (22). Contracting the two current sites and then applying the two-qubit gate gives every coefficient of the updated two-site tensor. Intervening bond weights are absorbed into the input sites.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2, Eqs. (21)–(27). A pivot-based split reconstructs every gate-transformed coefficient. Its minimal bond is exactly the updated coefficient-matrix rank; in independent outer frames this is the full physical cut rank.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2. With all three input bonds at most χ, contraction, gate application and exact pivot refactorization charge at most 24χ³+40χ² scalar operations and exact zero probes.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Eq. (4). An open n-site qubit chain of peak bond χ stores at most 2nχ² scalar tensor entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1, adjacent-gate execution. Updating any addressed pair and repeating for a finite sequence gives on every bit string exactly the amplitude obtained by dense evolution.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemmas 1–2 and Theorem 1. A G-gate adjacent sequence with actual peak virtual bond χ charges at most G(24χ³+40χ²) scalar operations. The physical Schmidt-rank justification is proved separately.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Paragraph preceding Theorem 1. Interleaved physical labels enumerate the bra/ket pairs. Cached matrix products and the one-site transfer implement the dense contraction for each local observable.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Paragraph preceding Theorem 1. Right-to-left contraction of any product of local qubit operators is equal to the full sum over pairs of physical basis strings. At maximum bond χ it uses at most n(20χ³+8χ²) scalar operations including conjugations.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2. Full rank gives injectivity of the linear maps from the virtual bond labels into a physical-site unfolding.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2, Eq. (22). Every two-site physical slice after the update is the prescribed linear combination of the pre-gate slices.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2. The rank-revealing split has full rank on both sides of the updated bond. For an invertible physical gate, the old slices can be recovered, which preserves the other two full-rank conditions. These facts maintain minimal reachable bond spaces under arbitrary gate order.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Eq. (2) and Lemma 2. The full-rank suffix and accumulated prefix frame recover the bond dimension from the physical flattening rank of the state at the current split.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Eq. (2) and Theorem 1. For a nonzero product input, invertible adjacent gates preserve full bond spaces. An intrinsic bound on all contiguous physical flattenings then bounds each computed bond throughout the trajectory.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1 for nearest-neighbour circuits. Starting with any product of nonzero local vectors and χ bounding the physical ranks at every dense circuit prefix, the explicit simulator reproduces all amplitudes and charges at most G(24χ³+40χ²) exact scalar operations and zero tests for G gates.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 2. The dense elimination schedule retains one pivot row and column per completed stage and recycles the residual matrix. Temporary tables for an addressed pair require at most 36χ²+4χ scalar slots.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1. The complete adjacent-gate simulation schedule, including the stored chain and temporary update tables, uses at most 2nχ²+36χ²+4χ scalar slots. The unit is a field coefficient; binary precision is outside this arithmetic model.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Paragraph preceding Theorem 1. Transfer contraction on the computed chain equals the product-observable expectation of the original dense circuit. Taking a local projector and identity on all other qubits gives its Born probability on a normalized input.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1. Simulation followed by a product-observable measurement costs at most G(24χ³+40χ²)+n(20χ³+8χ²) scalar operations, with G the number of adjacent gates and χ the computed peak width.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Paragraph preceding Theorem 1. The measurement sweep retains at most 7χ² temporary coefficient slots. Including the stored chain, the update temporaries, and the final observable contraction, the peak is at most 2nχ²+36χ²+4χ scalar slots, excluding read-only gate and observable inputs.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Lemma 1 and Theorem 1. A one-qubit register uses its ordinary matrix-vector product with eight scalar operations per gate. In a larger register, each one-site gate embeds exactly on adjacent wires as U⊗I or I⊗U; an inverse of U gives an inverse for the embedding.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Elementary Boolean arithmetic for Section 5 postprocessing. The six-gate conditional-complement ripple cell handles one bit of addition or subtraction. A width-w chain has 6w gates, an exact carry/borrow specification and a bounded result.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Executable candidate selection and comparison. Word equality uses 3w gates, a selector uses 4w, and checking the returned residue and updating the least positive candidate costs 24w gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The explicit shift-add multiplier with per-bit selection computes x·y when it fits in the w-bit result word and uses exactly 10wn Boolean gates for n multiplier bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The restoring divider computes quotient and remainder together in exactly 10wn Boolean gates for an n-bit dividend, positive divisor y and padded remainder width satisfying 2y ≤ 2^w.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The binary Euclidean recurrence returns precisely the Section 5 decoder’s numerator and denominator at index t, with at most 30(t+1)(w+1)² Boolean data-path operations on w-bit inputs.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Double-width schoolbook multiplication followed by restoring division gives modular reduction. Evaluating both possible square-and-multiply products at every exponent bit implements modular powering in at most 150n(w+1)² Boolean data-path operations.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Executable Boolean refinement of the continued-fraction and modular-verification decoder after Eq. (5.13). Its result exactly equals the natural-arithmetic decoder, with at most 1000(w+1)⁴ Boolean operations at w=1+floor(log₂ max(N,Q)). The branching computation treats shifts, indexing, wiring and fan-out as free. Gate counts come from explicit ripple, selection, multiplication and division routines.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"For the Section 5 continued-fraction decoder, the integer recurrence maintains coprimality, a positive denominator and bounded numerator/denominator. Computing index t takes at most t+1 quotient levels.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"At every index, including after an exact rational terminates, the Euclidean recurrence agrees with Mathlib’s Real.convergent. It already produces the reduced denominator, as needed for the order test after Eq. (5.13).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Invariant for the Section 5 implementation that keeps the least positive passing denominator. It updates the minimum when another candidate is inserted, using zero for the empty set.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Exact algorithmic refinement of Section 5 continued-fraction postprocessing. Scanning t integer convergents and testing denominators by binary modular exponentiation yields the reference minimum over verified real convergent denominators, or zero if there are none.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The actual scan counts its Euclidean frames and modular multiplications. For clock width L and budget 2L+1, their bounds are (2L+1)(2L+2) and 2L(2L+1), respectively. This supplies an explicit arithmetic-work bound for Section 5 postprocessing.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"At each visited Euclidean frame the operands and intermediate numerator products and sums are at most the clock size Q, and both unreduced products in each modular multiplication frame are below N². These width bounds complement the polynomial arithmetic counts.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"The spectral theorem, a reciprocal-square-root estimate and a matrix-product bound control the change of coefficients.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1747632","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Symmetric orthogonalization with the inverse square root of a positive overlap near the identity gives an exactly orthonormal orbital frame.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1747632","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The full overlap matrix determines the transformation. For spectral overlap error δ at most one half, the one- and two-electron integral perturbations cost at most (1+2δ)²−1 and (1+2δ)⁴−1 times their respective spectral norms.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1747632","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For two commuting finite complex matrices the product of their exponentials, repeated n times, exactly exponentiates n times their sum. Lloyd also treats noncommuting local terms with approximation error.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.273.5278.1073"]},{"scope":"Two bounded generators in a complete unital real normed algebra obey the Lie–Trotter limit in norm. Trotter’s semigroup theorem also allows generators with domain conditions.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9939-1959-0108732-6"]},{"scope":"Telescoping bounds the norm difference of nth powers in terms of the one-step difference. For two contractions the factor is at most n.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9939-1959-0108732-6"]},{"scope":"Bounds the norm of the algebra exponential by exp of the argument norm in the bounded-generator proof.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9939-1959-0108732-6"]},{"scope":"Finite matrix identities for the controlled SELECT operation and its conjugation by a unitary ancilla preparation. They certify unitarity and one joint matrix element.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"In Eqs. (7)–(11), the reference ancilla block of PREPARE† SELECT PREPARE equals the sum of term unitaries weighted by prepared probabilities. Probabilities cₖ/α give H/α, as in the paper’s LCU construction.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"The squared weight in the reference ancilla sector is ‖Hψ‖²/α². This is a success probability for a unit system input and is derived from the circuit block.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"An orthogonal projector gives an involutory reflection. A product of two symmetric involutions preserves the complex inner product, as required by the signal walk.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The compressed powers of the walk obey a second-order operator recurrence for a symmetric involutory oracle and an orthogonal signal projector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For the Hermitian involution encoding, compression of the nth walk power gives the Chebyshev polynomial of the signal. A signal eigenvector of value λ acquires the projected amplitude Tₙ(λ), or cos(n arccos λ) for a unit eigenvector. This realizes the signal dynamics of Theorem 2.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A real eigenvalue of the signal on a normalized encoded eigenvector has absolute value at most one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For a unit encoded eigenvector the state-vector error of a finite weighted sum of projected walks is the scalar approximation error at its eigenvalue.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A degree-two walk polynomial approximates the real-time phase on a unit encoded eigenvector for |t| ≤ 1. Lean gives the explicit error 2|tλ|³/9 for this polynomial.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The first k exponential Taylor terms have norm error at most eˢsᵏ/k! at s = ‖x‖, and converge in a complete normed algebra. These estimates support the segment analysis.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"The proof controls the perturbation of repeated powers near a contraction and identifies the norm of the scaled generator −ihH.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"For a bounded Hamiltonian the one-segment truncation has a factorial remainder. For self-adjoint H and r positive equal segments the operator error is bounded by (1+δ)ʳ−1, with a corresponding bound on input vectors. Circuit synthesis and query cost require additional results.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.114.090502"]},{"scope":"Differentiating energy invariance under a linear isometry gives the directional force transformation at a differentiable configuration. The cited work develops a gradient-domain force-field model.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1603015"]},{"scope":"The matrix amplification acting on system blocks equals conjugation and summation with the ancilla-extended Kraus matrices.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0024-3795(75)90075-0","https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(71)90108-4"]},{"scope":"The finite Kraus representation gives a complex linear map preserving positivity at every finite ancilla size. This is the representation-to-complete-positivity direction of Choi’s characterization.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0024-3795(75)90075-0"]},{"scope":"Kraus completeness preserves trace with an arbitrary finite ancilla. Combined with complete positivity it preserves normalized positive joint states.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(71)90108-4","https://doi.org/10.1016/0024-3795(75)90075-0"]},{"scope":"For a finite family of matrices, the completeness relation ΣK†K=I makes the operator sum trace preserving.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(71)90108-4"]},{"scope":"The trace of one finite-matrix Lindblad dissipator vanishes by cyclicity. The generator characterization in the paper is a stronger statement.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01608499"]},{"scope":"For one nuclear coordinate on a differentiable potential surface, Newton’s law implies zero instantaneous derivative of kinetic plus potential energy. Existence and accuracy of a molecular adiabatic approximation are additional questions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19273892002"]},{"scope":"When clusters are labeled finite subsets and a weight is the observable minus all proper subcluster weights, Boolean-lattice Möbius inversion recovers that weight. This models the linked-cluster subtraction step.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.97.187202"]},{"scope":"A cyclic trace identity exchanges propagation of a finite matrix state with propagation of the observable. This is a mathematical prerequisite for the Heisenberg-picture algorithm.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.6.020302"]},{"scope":"Conjugating an anticommuting string Q by a rotation generated by P with P²=I gives two terms in Q and PQ. The trigonometric rotation follows by choosing its half-angle coefficients.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.6.020302"]},{"scope":"An exact matrix intertwiner HQ=QK propagates to all powers HᵏQ=QKᵏ.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.451548"]},{"scope":"If QQ† fixes every Krylov vector through degree d, lifting the dth power of Q†HQ reproduces Hᵈv. The paper uses the projected operator for time propagation.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.451548"]},{"scope":"For finite complex trajectory weights of nonzero total weight, sampling with magnitudes and restoring the phase gives the exact reweighted ratio. The average phase has modulus at most one.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.79.035320"]},{"scope":"For a finite permutation of beads, the sum of harmonic nearest-neighbor spring forces vanishes. This is internal-force cancellation in the ring-polymer model.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1777575"]},{"scope":"For an everywhere-defined bounded self-adjoint Hamiltonian, exp(−itH) is a unitary one-parameter group and is differentiable with the Schrödinger generator. Stone also characterizes general strongly continuous groups via possibly unbounded generators.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.2307/1968538"]},{"scope":"The symmetric product VTV has (VTV)†(VTV)=I when each factor does. This verifies norm preservation of a split step and leaves splitting error as a separate estimate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9991(82)90091-2"]},{"scope":"In a fixed real basis with symmetric linear H, the real and imaginary TD-CI component equations imply zero derivative of their total squared norm.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1999636"]},{"scope":"In a real inner-product space any tangent trial velocity whose residual is orthogonal to the tangent subspace minimizes squared residual over that subspace.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1080/00268976400100041"]},{"scope":"A finite weighted sum of outer products of centered real log-derivative samples has nonnegative quadratic form when the weights are nonnegative. This supplies a sampled variational metric property.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/srep00243"]},{"scope":"Differentiating the subtractive two-layer ONIOM energy gives full-low force plus model-high force minus model-low force along one coordinate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/jp962071j"]},{"scope":"For pair-additive dimer energies the two-body fragment expansion equals the sum of monomer energies and pair corrections. Equality assumes the stated pair model.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/s0009-2614(99)00874-x"]},{"scope":"Restricting the Coulomb constrained search to active densities plus a fixed environment gives an upper bound on the library’s molecular trial-class infimum. Attainment by a compatible split gives equality.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/j100132a040","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062"]},{"scope":"Given error bounds on learned high-minus-low energy corrections at two configurations, the gap error is at most the sum of the endpoint bounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.5b00099"]},{"scope":"For finite supported interaction terms, regional inclusion–exclusion plus terms crossing the two regions reconstructs the union energy. This is an accounting identity for a specified decomposition.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4742816"]},{"scope":"Along a scalar density variation, the derivative of total minus active minus constant frozen energy equals the difference of the first two derivatives.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/j100132a040"]},{"scope":"In an additive density model each active-plus-frozen split must be feasible. Restricting the infimum gives an upper bound, with equality if a split realizes the unrestricted infimum.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/j100132a040","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062"]},{"scope":"Assuming z−D invertible on the eliminated sector, a nonzero eigenvector of a block operator is equivalent to a nonzero solution of the effective equation with unique reconstructed environment component.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(58)90007-1"]},{"scope":"For a shared positive rate prefactor, nonnegative inverse temperature β and a barrier error at most ε, the predicted/exact exponential rate ratio lies between exp(−βε) and exp(βε).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1749604"]},{"scope":"At fixed geometry the ONIOM solver error equals the high-level imaginary-time energy excess. A finite gapped model with initial ground overlap gives pointwise convergence and, with an excitation cutoff, an exponential upper bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/jp962071j","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-019-0704-4"]},{"scope":"The derivative of a normalized differentiable eigenbranch of a locally symmetric bounded Hamiltonian supplies the high-level force in the one-coordinate ONIOM surface.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/jp962071j","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.56.340"]},{"scope":"On a one-coordinate Newton trajectory driven by the eigenbranch force and both MM forces, kinetic plus layered electronic potential energy has zero derivative.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/jp962071j","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.56.340","https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19273892002"]},{"scope":"For a nonempty finite canonical ensemble and β>0, the difference of free energies is −β⁻¹ log of the reference expectation of exp(−βΔU).","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1740409"]},{"scope":"For a 1D uniform grid with homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries and mode index 1 through N, the sine mode is nonzero and diagonalizes the second-difference stencil with its explicit eigenvalue.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1088/1367-2630/15/1/013021"]},{"scope":"Given finitely many eigenvectors with nonzero eigenvalues, multiplying their reciprocally weighted superposition by A recovers the input superposition. HHL’s approximation, success probability and complexity need further proofs.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Squared branch norms ‖Kᵢψ‖² are nonnegative and sum to one for a normalized input under Kraus completeness. This extends the probability rule to a finite generalized measurement.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01397477","https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(71)90108-4"]},{"scope":"Given orthogonal idempotent branch operators, conditioning on a positive-probability projection yields a unit state with probability one of the same outcome on repetition.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19504430510"]},{"scope":"For a normalized qubit and diagonal weak branches of strength η in [−1,1], Lean computes the branch probabilities and posterior z polarization. This is an explicit measurement example in the setting of weak-measurement decomposition.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.95.110409"]},{"scope":"Finite Fourier orthogonality yields unit readout amplitude on the exact eigenphase grid point and zero at every other grid point. Off-grid phase error is a separate analysis.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"For a differentiable eigenbranch in a real Hilbert space and bounded differentiable H, normalization and symmetry at the evaluation point give E′=⟨ψ,H′ψ⟩.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.56.340"]},{"scope":"For known distinct real energies, exact samples at n uniformly spaced imaginary times with positive spacing identify n discrete amplitudes by a Vandermonde argument. Silver et al. study ill-conditioned noisy analytic continuation.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.41.2380"]},{"scope":"A real-coefficient finite Pauli decomposition reconstructs the Hamiltonian expectation as the coefficient-weighted sum of measured term expectations.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213"]},{"scope":"For equally many calibrated ±1 shots per term with pairwise independence, Lean proves unbiasedness and bounds the energy-estimator variance by Σcᵢ²/n. The statistics lemma also gives the exact variance.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213"]},{"scope":"Computational-basis identities connect the repository’s Pauli expansion to normalized traces against Pauli strings and linear combinations. These support the evaluation in Appendix D, equations (D.7)–(D.8).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Conjugation by a single-site Z multiplies a Pauli word by −1 when the word carries X or Y there and by +1 otherwise. This identifies the sum in equation (D.8).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Equation (D.7). For a Hermitian involutory n-qubit butterfly the squared real coefficients in the concrete Pauli basis sum to one.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Equation (D.8). For a Hermitian involutory evolved butterfly, its normalized first-order OTOC with local Z equals one minus twice the squared Pauli weight of words that anticommute with Z.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Dropping words only from the final sum in (D.8) overestimates the exact OTOC by twice their discarded anticommuting weight. The propagation error of the paper’s window algorithm requires Theorem 1.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"The unnormalized trace of (A ⊗ B) times the global swap equals Tr(AB). This contraction identity underlies the two-copy readout.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Equation (III.2). The normalized four-point trace equals the normalized two-copy trace of the evolved butterfly tensored with itself, the doubled probe and the global swap. This is the readout used for window propagation.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Appendix A, Fact 1. Conjugation by U ⊗ U fixes both identity and swap, here for any finite-dimensional unitary U. These invariant tails are used in the window representation.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24640"]},{"scope":"Theorem 17, Eq. (32). For a finite-dimensional projected unitary encoding and a phase list realizing P, compressing the alternating word gives the odd or even singular-value transform of the encoded matrix. Orthonormal frames represent the two projector ranges. The result includes the empty phase word.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01838"]},{"scope":"Corollary 8 and Theorem 17, Eq. (32). For a complex polynomial of positive degree d satisfying the paper’s parity and magnitude conditions, d real phases realize P exactly and its alternating unitary word implements the singular-value transform on the specified input and output spaces.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01838"]},{"scope":"The two cases of Theorem 17, Eq. (32). The compressed alternating unitary word transforms the singular values by the response polynomial of a prescribed odd or even phase list. The even word uses the adjoint oracle and both outer projections onto the input space.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01838"]},{"scope":"Corollary 8, Eq. (14). The response polynomial of a length-d list of real phases equals a prescribed positive-degree complex polynomial under the parity and magnitude hypotheses. Qatlib also proves minimal length and parity for any realizing list.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01838"]},{"scope":"The identity word is I. Each Pauli word is Hermitian and squares to I, and each nonidentity Pauli has zero trace. These facts justify the hard density matrices of Supplementary Definition 2.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"The squared Pauli trace correlations of a Hermitian E sum to 2ⁿ Tr(E²). For a positive measurement effect E, its purity is bounded by Tr(E)².","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"There are (2ⁿ−1)(2ⁿ+1) nonidentity Pauli words on n qubits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Eq. (D35) implies a uniform mean square of 1/(2ⁿ+1) for pure measurement effects. This proof by Pauli Parseval gives an upper bound of 1/(2ⁿ+1) for the normalized trace responses of effects of arbitrary rank when n>0. Zero effects have zero responses.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Every normalized trace response of a positive measurement effect to a Pauli word lies in [−1,1].","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Definition 2. For nonidentity P and a real bias |a|≤1, ρ=(I+aP)/2ⁿ is a positive trace-one matrix with Tr(ρP)=a. The null, plus and minus hypotheses take a=0, +0.9, −0.9.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Each node chooses a finite POVM. Its Born weights sum to one on any trace-one input and the products of the successive Born weights form a normalized law over complete adaptive transcripts.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (D14)–(D20). A hard-state outcome weight is its maximally mixed weight times 1+a·Tr(EP)/Tr(E), with the zero effect handled explicitly. Multiplication along the observed path gives the leaf likelihood.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"All null and alternative leaf masses are nonnegative. The null law is independent of the Pauli revealed after acquisition.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"A direct inequality for the fair-sign mixture replaces the logarithmic estimate in Eqs. (D21)–(D34). Its likelihood relative to the null law is at least one minus the sum of squared local biased responses along the leaf.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"At additive tolerance 1/4 the intervals around 0 and 0.9 are disjoint. For every prediction, the sum of the two hypothesis error masses is bounded below using the sign mixture.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 6, conventional lower bound. Every finite-outcome adaptive single-copy POVM tree with a post-reveal prediction rule and error probability ≤1/5 under Definition 2 must use T≥20(2ⁿ+1)/27 copies, for n≥1. The proof gives its own constant.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"The same exponential lower bound holds for randomized adaptive strategies, averaging over any independent probability law on classical seeds. Each seed may select both the entire POVM tree and the post-reveal rule. The averaged failure probability must be at most 1/5.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1 and Supplementary Theorem 6. On the hard prior the two-copy Bell estimator uses 2048 pairs for error at most 1/4 with failure probability ≤1/5, uniformly in n. At the same accuracy, every randomized adaptive single-copy POVM learner needs at least 20(2ⁿ+1)/27 copies. The finite-outcome measurement and post-reveal access models match Supplementary Definitions 1–2.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn7293"]},{"scope":"Sections 2–3, equal-prior single-marked phase-oracle search. For every nonempty finite database, normalized initial workspace, oracle-independent gates and projective readout, average Born success up to the first maximum is bounded by the success of the explicit Grover circuit with the same query count.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.60.2746"]},{"scope":"Sharp sine-squared success upper bound through the first maximum and its attainment by computational-basis marking, uniform preparation and one common diffusion reflection per call. For at least two items the construction gives the sine law at every iteration.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.60.2746"]},{"scope":"If equal-prior success tends to one, then for every positive ε, all sufficiently large N require at least (π/4−ε)√N queries. The algorithm and workspace may vary with N.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.60.2746"]},{"scope":"The angle of the square root of average success is at most (2T+1) arcsin(1/√N), via a direct sum over candidate labels, the identity-oracle trajectory and a reflection about the readout sectors.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.60.2746"]},{"scope":"Hilbert direct-sum geometry, Born mass and reflections used in the upper bound, plus the computational basis, normalized uniform state and explicit Grover iterate used for equality.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.60.2746"]},{"scope":"A coordinate of the K-by-N product law has the original one-shot marginal; this constructs the independent sampling model in Supplementary Theorem 1.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Under independence, Nε²≥34V and single-shot variance at most V give block failure probability at most 1/34 by Chebyshev.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"An upper median that misses the target has at least ⌈K/2⌉ bad blocks. For independent block estimates with failure probabilities at most 1/34, the median fails with probability at most 2⁻ᴷ, for odd and even K.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Rounding K=2 ln(2M/δ) upward gives a positive block count for M≥1 and 0\u003cδ≤1; M·2⁻ᴷ≤δ.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Median-of-means concentration in Supplementary Theorem 1 for arbitrary square-integrable real scores, sharing the same records across all targets. The means and variance bounds are parameters of this statistical lemma.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Unitary-basis Born projectors are Hermitian, have trace one, and sum to the identity in each basis. Each unitary entry has norm at most one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Entrywise measurability of the unitary matrices gives measurable trace scores; uniform entry bounds supply finite second moments for arbitrary probability measures on the bases.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The outcome-projector channel of Eq. (S2) is unital, symmetric under the trace pairing and adjoint preserving. Its inverse preserves Hermitian observables.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"For Eq. (S3), applying the inverse channel to the rank-one snapshot or to the observable gives the same trace score. For Hermitian observables the score is real.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Eq. (S5). The Born outcome law and invertibility of the channel imply the mean of the inverse-snapshot estimator is the desired trace expectation.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Eq. (S8). Subtracting the normalized trace of the observable shifts every individual score by that same scalar.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Eq. (S7), before maximizing over states. The squared real score integrates to the ensemble average of the Born-weighted squared matrix element of the inverse observable, including continuous basis ensembles.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"The one-state second moment is bounded by its supremum over all density states. Bounded scores and the normalization of every Born law give a finite upper bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Lemma 1, Eq. (S6). The variance of the actual one-shot score is bounded by the squared shadow norm after subtracting the observable’s scalar part.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1 with the constants of Eq. (S13), on any probability space realizing the independent shared Born records. The basis ensemble may be continuous. Each block uses at least one shot when every centered norm is zero.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Supplementary Theorem 1, Eqs. (S7), (S12)–(S14). From the NK-fold product of the Born law, the medians predict every Hermitian observable simultaneously with failure at most δ. K=⌈2 ln(2M/δ)⌉ and N=max(1,⌈34 maxᵢ‖Oᵢ−Tr(Oᵢ)I/d‖²shadow/ε²⌉).","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-020-0932-7"]},{"scope":"Two Euclidean remainder steps halve the denominator. Starting below 2^k, the algorithm performs at most 2k divisions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172"]},{"scope":"Identifies termination of the real continued fraction for a measured rational o/Q with an explicit Euclidean division count. The scan over the first 2(log₂ Q + 1) + 1 indices suffices for every o \u003c Q, with the logarithm rounded down.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The explicit logarithmic scan satisfies ForShor’s continued-fraction completeness specification for every clock width.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The ideal initialized Fourier circuit and the bounded order decoder together return the exact order with Born probability at least 4φ(r)/(π²r), hence at least φ(r)/(3r). ForShor chooses N² \u003c Q ≤ 2N². This includes the paper’s clock for moduli which are not powers of two.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"After ceil(12 ln(1+floor(log₂(r+1)))) independent trials, the failure bound obtained from the φ(r)/(3r) certificate is at most exp(−1).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Finite matrix semantics of the reference two-limb Toom–Cook implementation. On the all-zero input, at precision chosen for the modulus and with fqchem’s bounded decoder, its one-run success probability is at least a strictly positive certified bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The checked interpolation table gives an admissible constant lowering policy for the modular arithmetic circuit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The submitted low-level circuit achieves the reference bound using its allocated layout, selected precision, all-zero input, and any complete continued-fraction scan.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"There is enough precision for the reference circuit to receive a positive probability certificate at every modulus.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"An explicit lower bound for the unit fraction φ(r)/r, logarithmic in the bit width, supplies the independent-retry estimate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Preparing the zero registers and applying Hadamards and modular exponentiation yields the pre-inverse-QFT state used in Section 5.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"There are at least φ(r) distinct good clock outcomes and each has probability at least 4/(π²r). Summation gives the order-recovery mass.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For the initialized ideal circuit the Born distribution agrees with the finite exponential sums of Section 5.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The register bounds imply r² \u003c Q and every good outcome passes the order check within a complete scan.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For a circuit within its qubit ceiling, the abstract Born probability on zero input equals the finite-dimensional circuit matrix probability.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"An executable positive-denominator scan using binary modular multiplication returns exactly the same natural number as ForShor’s specification.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"At the explicit integer precision schedule the number of extra modular work bits is bounded by 70 times the data width.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For the two-limb reference multiplication table and explicit precision level, the same circuit evaluated by programRecovery uses O(n³) logical gates at data width n, in the ForShor elementary-gate model.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The explicit two-limb circuit on the zero vector, measured in its finite matrix semantics, has success at least 1/(2π² ln(1+floor(log₂ N))). Its executable decoder and precision schedule are fixed.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The executable precision level 2²⁶n⁴ gives enough accuracy for the lowered circuit to keep half the ideal success certificate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Summation of Born weights over clock outcomes returning a verified factor gives a nonnegative result.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For each good unit the circuit’s exact-order outcomes yield genuine factors after the executable gcd check. Thus the one-shot factor probability is at least the quantum recovery certificate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Every clock outcome of the physical low-level matrix circuit has nonnegative Born weight.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Averaging the physical matrix Born probability over a uniformly chosen unit proves an unconditional one-run lower bound (1−2^(1−k))/(2π² ln(1+floor(log₂ N))) for odd N>1 with k distinct primes.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Converting the chosen unit to the input structure preserves the multiplicative order used by the quantum circuit.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Computes the extra register bits at an integer reference precision in terms of the natural ceiling logarithm.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The reference allocator sets the data register width to floor(log₂(2N)).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The reference allocator sets the clock width to floor(log₂(2N²)).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"At sufficiently fine approximation precision the complete lowered circuit keeps half the reference success bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"An admissible Toom–Cook interpolation table with bounded exponent gives the logical gate-count bound for all ready layouts and linearly many extra work bits.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The constant 1/π² in the compiled success bound is positive.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The reciprocal precision needed for a valid order-finding layout grows at most quartically with data width.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"An initialized ready layout supplies the workspace required by the compiler.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Taking the least nonzero executable candidate returns the true order whenever at least one trial recovers it. Every positive candidate passes modular verification and hence is at least the order.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"At Shor’s binary clock, the wrong-answer probability of the independent Fourier experiments and bounded decoder is ≤1/e in ceil(12 ln(1+floor(log₂(r+1)))) trials. The joint law is the product of the normalized matrix Born distributions.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Each clock outcome has nonnegative weight in the explicit projected matrix column.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Summing over the complete clock register gives probability one. The circuit matrix is unitary, the input is the zero basis vector, and the register projectors partition the final amplitudes.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For an arbitrary positive base, the stochastic output measure counts exactly the verified factor probability, including the immediate gcd branch.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For any composite m ≤ N, preprocessing and one fair-word trial return a checked proper divisor with probability at least 1/[128(1+floor(log₂ N))].","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Summation over uniformly sampled fair words accounts for rejected draws; one physical trial keeps at least half the mean accepted-base factor probability.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Two order-finding inputs with the same base and modulus describe the same quantum experiment.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The finite-matrix circuit, bit decoder, classical gcd check and recursive splitter return primes multiplying to N with probability at least 2/3. The product is exact on every tape and the bound on trial count is cubic in the input bit length.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Every accepted factor in the pushed-forward clock distribution satisfies both strict bounds and divides the modulus.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Pushing the normalized clock measure through the executable decoder and factor extractor recovers its explicit finite Born sum.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The fair-word sample space has between N−1 and 2(N−1) elements, so at least half the words encode usable positive bases.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Whenever the verified extractor accepts the true order, every circuit outcome decoding that order returns the certified divisor.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"A uniform draw from all integers 1,…,N−1 and one compiled experiment yield a factor with probability at least (1−2^(1−k))/(2π² ln(1+floor(log₂ N))). This includes nonunits, for which the first gcd already splits N.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Converting a positive coprime base less than N to a modular unit preserves its natural representative.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Every Fourier phase has unit modulus, as needed for normalization of the clock transform.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"An interval-allocated register has exactly its declared binary width.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Every positive exponent passing modular verification is at least the multiplicative order.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"A near-peak outcome for an index coprime to the order passes the complete continued-fraction decoder.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The finite matrix of the lowered circuit is unitary at every qubit dimension large enough for its gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The register projector keeps exactly the amplitudes whose measured bits encode the requested outcome.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The integer obtained by reading the register bits is smaller than the register basis size.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The implemented success coordinate agrees with the signed reciprocal on regular bins. The decoded phase distance bounds the relative error even across a sign change.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The exact sine clock on either spectral sign has squared relative error of order 1/(dM)² for any superposition. For a normalized input the selected branch has mass at least 4d² and normalized vector error at most 160/(dM), preserved by unitary uncomputation.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Perturbation bound for normalized successful branches.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Legality of the canonical signed filter and its action on an initialized flag qudit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The sine-clock distribution is normalized and satisfies a circular second-moment bound independent of the eigenphase.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The inverse Fourier transform, controlled-time phases and sine-state preparation give the exact eigenphase kernel. Their adjoint uncomputes the reciprocal-weighted state to a zero clock. The signed solution branch has probability at least 4d² and normalized error at most 160/(dM).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The initialized sine-envelope clock has unit norm and is prepared by a unitary.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The controlled-time phases and quantum Fourier transform are unitary.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The direct sum of unitary eigenphase-clock blocks is unitary on the combined register.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The unitary composes the preparation, forward clock, signed reciprocal rotation and inverse clock. Its selected amplitudes and Born mass agree with the analyzed solution branch.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Unknown-success amplitude amplification of this signed solver succeeds with probability at least 1/8 averaged over the chosen depths. Its conditional solution is preserved up to global phase, and the solver and inverse calls are counted.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502","https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"The fixed forward and adjoint clock words use controlled evolutions and return their work bit on arbitrary input clock states.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"For 4d ≤ |λ| ≤ 1/4, the signed solver attains vector error ε and constant success with at most 2/d input-preparation or inverse calls and 640/(d²ε) exact controlled evolutions. Elementary sparse runtime requires additional synthesis.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502","https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"The uniform forward and adjoint words query the evolution in the original Hermitian matrix basis. The eigenvector transformation is used to prove their action on every clock state.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"For an invertible finite Hermitian matrix of either spectral sign in the stated bands, the clean-clock target is the ordinary inverse applied to the input in its original basis. At clock length M the normalized successful state has vector error at most 160/(dM) and unamplified probability at least 4d².","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Taking an adjoint preserves the evolution query count.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"A randomized window of Grover depths bounds the mean success probability and preserves the selected output up to global phase.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502","https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Conjugating the diagonal eigenphase evolution gives a unitary input oracle.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The sine-clock word is unitary and makes exactly M−1 controlled-evolution calls.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The amplification word separately counts its preparation and adjoint uses.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Operational execution and matrix evaluation agree for the forward word and its unitary adjoint.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"The clock threshold loop applies each bin’s controlled power and restores the work bit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Fix all widths except n in a function oracle. Hardwiring the off-width answers gives a width-n adaptive decision tree with the same Boolean output and at most the original number of queries. State labels are inaccessible to the algorithm.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Execution depends only on the oracle at widths the tree can reach. Success probability additionally uses the target width's correct label. These statements preserve earlier diagonal requirements while later widths are set.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.3, applied to the restriction of a randomized adaptive tree: for n ≥ 64 and at most 2^(n/4) queries, some promised replacement at width n reduces success below 2/3, even with arbitrary fixed values at other widths.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, §3.2. One function oracle defeats every member of a countable collection of randomized adaptive query algorithms infinitely often. Each program may query across lengths, with any given finite width budget and at most 2^(n/4) calls. The amplified quantum procedure succeeds with probability at least 7/8 at all n ≥ 64.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"The finite segment of the physical query tape grows by at most one cell per local machine step. Its binary query ends at the first blank, and a length-preserving oracle reply occupies exactly that many cells.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Fix the random tape of the two-tape probabilistic oracle machine. Its finite adaptive query tree returns the machine's direct operational result for every fixed oracle, including cross-width queries. Local steps pass the clock without consuming a query.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, §3.2. A single fixed promised oracle gives amplified quantum success ≥ 7/8 from width 64 and defeats each finite two-tape probabilistic oracle machine with probability > 1/3 on arbitrarily large unary inputs under the floor(2^(n/4)) step clock. The paper schedule uses 3n² sampling queries and 6n³ local gates.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"On unary strings the target bit is the oracle's permutation-versus-hidden-period label. The amplified Born law evaluated at that bit equals Qatlib's two-sided success quantity by normalization of the Boolean output.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"A uniform schedule of n independent blocks of 3n samples uses polynomially many operations: the actual Hadamard lists, oracle calls, measurements, bit conversion, counted binary Gaussian elimination on arbitrary sample rows, and final candidate scan fit in 100(n+1)⁴ operations.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"For each real constant C, any integer time bound at most C 2^(n/8) eventually fits in the diagonalization clock floor(2^(n/4)).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, Theorem 3.4, for a length-preserving function oracle. One oracle and unary language admit ≥ 7/8 quantum success on every binary input via the uniform Simon circuit and a fixed 64-entry small-input table. Quantum work is polynomial. Every finite-table two-tape fair-coin oracle Turing machine under the floor(2^(n/4)) clock has success \u003c 2/3 on arbitrarily large unary inputs, even with adaptive queries at other widths.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Probability helper: pointwise equal score functions have equal expectation under a discrete law. It transfers machine execution equivalences through random choices.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Probability helper: the expectation of a score under a pushed-forward discrete law equals its expectation after composition. It transfers Simon success probabilities through transcript compilation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"The counted binary Gaussian decoder returns the specified Simon-secret decoder’s answer and uses at most 10(mn²+n³+mn+n²+n+1) bit operations on m input rows of width n.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Simon, §3.2. At width n ≥ 64 every randomized classical width-n tree of at most 2^(n/4) queries has success below 2/3 on some promised labeled oracle.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Enumeration helper: the first component of the natural-number pairing bijection takes each machine index at arbitrarily large integers. The diagonal proof tests every machine infinitely often.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"On every injective or strict nonzero-period oracle of width n ≥ 64 the amplified Simon schedule has success at least 7/8. This is the quantum upper bound used after diagonalization.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Normalization of the two Boolean probabilities of the amplified Simon decision law lets success be read directly at the correct label.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"The explicit concatenated local gate program has the gate count in the ledger. The other entries count sampled queries, measurements and conversion steps for r blocks of n+k samples.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"At the fixed schedule k = 2n and r = n, the local Simon experiment ledger has 3n² queries, 3n² measurements, 6n³ local gates and 3n³ bit conversion steps.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539796298637"]},{"scope":"Each coordinate of a q-query circuit’s output amplitude has real and imaginary polynomial representatives of degree at most q (Lemma 4.1).","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"Distinguishing the two parity classes around any threshold requires N/4 coherent whole-row queries, even when the separation is arbitrarily small.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"At arbitrary time t, the evolved endpoint on the selected parity track has Born probability |sin(t/2)|²ᴺ. An estimator accurate to less than half this signal needs at least N/4 row queries.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"The endpoint signal before perfect transfer bounds the factorial cutoff using the exponential series and a uniform lower bound on sine.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For coherent access to two incident edge bits per row, any q-query approximation to the evolved endpoint at all scaled times through τ, with absolute probability error ε\u003c1/2, admits a factorial cutoff at most 32q+8.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"A single phase oracle encodes the mean of N signs. The parity Fourier coefficient of its evolution can be evaluated exactly, while it vanishes in any compressed circuit amplitude with fewer than N queries.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163","https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049"]},{"scope":"On these explicitly block-encoded inputs, q controlled phase queries at time budget τ and uniform compressed-amplitude error ε\u003c1 force the least admissible factorial cutoff in Eq. (1) to be at most 8(q+1). The simulator may choose its workspace, input and output frame.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"The explicit literal-conflict graph on three vertices per clause has an independent set of size equal to the number of clauses exactly when the input formula is satisfiable.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Starting with any nonempty 3-CNF, the compact-orbital Hamiltonian gives a low-energy Slater witness on yes instances and an inverse-polynomially separated bound for every normalized fixed-number state on no instances.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For a finite mode space, conjugating the occupation-basis amplitudes of an exterior determinant gives its physical bra on every state. Orthonormal occupied orbitals give norm one, as in O’Gorman et al., Eq. (14).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.34.1293","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The overlap with the exterior power of an identity plus m coordinate moves equals an m-by-m determinant of shifted one-particle density entries. The finite-rank identity supplies the one- and two-body contractions.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.34.1293"]},{"scope":"For an orthonormal complex occupied frame, the one-body expectation is the density entry and the two-body expectation is its antisymmetrized two-by-two minor. These are number-conserving two- and four-operator cases of Wick contraction on a single determinant.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.80.268","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.34.1293"]},{"scope":"A coordinate move has support on its source mode. Multilinearity expands a pair of occupied-slot replacements, and permuting the two moves preserves their joint two-body operator.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.36.1121"]},{"scope":"Condon, §4. Acting on an ordered occupation determinant replaces one occupied source or two distinct occupied sources. Attempting to annihilate an absent source or a repeated source gives zero. Exterior antisymmetry supplies the signs upon reordering.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.36.1121"]},{"scope":"Slater, Part 2, and O’Gorman et al., Eqs. (3)–(5) and (14). For arbitrary finite complex one- and two-body coefficients, a normalized determinant has energy given by contracting its one-particle density and its direct-minus-exchange minor. The two-body coefficient includes the Hamiltonian’s double-counting factor.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.34.1293","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Finite-dimensional estimates relate the entrywise orbital and Gram checks to the Hilbert-space norm and operator norm. The orbital-frame columns satisfy the orthonormality condition of Eq. (14) exactly when their Gram matrix is the identity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For two frames with entry moduli bounded by two and one- and two-body integral moduli bounded by M, an entrywise perturbation ε changes the complex score by at most (4d²Mk + 64d⁴Mk²)ε. Here d counts modes and k counts occupied orbitals.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Löwdin symmetric orthonormalization corrects an approximately orthonormal complex d-by-k orbital matrix to an exact occupied frame. If every input entry has modulus at most two and each Gram residual is at most z with k²z ≤ 1/2, the change per entry is at most 4k³z.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1747632","https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For the certificate in §IV A, rounding each real and imaginary component on the 1/D grid takes two signed integers per orbital coefficient. It introduces at most 2/D entrywise error and at most 8d/D error per Gram entry.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For bounded integrals and a positive inverse promise gap, rounding a low-energy determinant supplies a passing finite grid certificate. Every accepted certificate yields an exactly normalized Slater determinant below the NO threshold after Gram correction. This proves the numerical witness implications used in the membership part of Theorem 3.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Exact integer scaling recovers each decoded orbital, its one-particle density, and its Gram residual. Comparing a Gaussian integer’s squared norm with (8dD)² implements the Gram test in §IV A’s finite witness model.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"With integral numerators over a common positive denominator C and orbital coordinates on a 1/D grid, the exact integer score has denominator CD⁴. The Boolean acceptance test agrees with the real Gram, component, and energy conditions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Two offset-encoded bit words represent each occupied orbital coefficient. If d, k, the magnitude bound M, and the inverse gap t are each at most 2ᵇ, every word has at most 13 + 12b bits. This quantifies §IV A’s polynomial-length Slater witness.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The finite-bit Boolean verifier accepts some witness in every YES instance and rejects every witness in every NO instance for general complex one- and two-body integrals with Gaussian integer numerators over one positive denominator. The promise concerns the exact expectation on orthonormal exterior determinants.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"An entrywise coefficient error of ε in the one- and two-body terms changes any normalized determinant’s expectation by at most (d²k + 2d⁴k²)ε. This transfers the precision of the coefficients in Eqs. (6)–(8) to the energy decision.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For physical coefficients approximated by the verifier’s rational input within a quarter of the promised energy gap after error propagation, the verifier preserves both decisions. It doubles the inverse-gap parameter and shifts the rational acceptance threshold. The input supplies certified coefficient approximations, as motivated by §II A’s efficient-integral assumption.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"A Gaussian integer’s component magnitudes obey sum and product bounds. Each operand throughout an arithmetic expression with s input and operation nodes has at most bs magnitude bits when its inputs satisfy the b-bit bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Recursive finite sums compile the density, Gram residual, and full direct-minus-exchange score into division-free Gaussian integer arithmetic. Expanding all sums gives polynomial operation bounds in d and k, including recomputation of density entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The compiled integer comparisons implement the component, Gram, and threshold tests of the executable verifier. Cross-multiplication uses the positive grid and common denominators. Boolean conjunction gives exactly the same answer.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"The entire family of 2dk + k² + 1 comparisons uses a polynomial number of input reads, Gaussian integer arithmetic operations, and scalar comparisons. Both the bound and the expression evaluator are explicit. This count begins with the integer input words and precomputed grid and Lipschitz parameters.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"For a b-bit bound on the encoded inputs and two precomputed integer parameters, every signed Gaussian-integer component in every comparison has polynomially many magnitude bits. This includes all intermediate products. The verified work model counts bounded-width arithmetic operations.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/prxquantum.3.020322"]},{"scope":"Proposition 14.2, pp. 142–143. For a positive contraction H, the positive-square-root outcome operators have effects H and I−H, the binary POVM in the local verification step.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Proposition 14.2, p. 143. Choosing a term uniformly among r positive contractions gives Born acceptance probability 1−⟨ψ,Hψ⟩/r on every normalized witness ψ, where H is the sum of the terms.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Proposition 14.2, pp. 142–143. In finite dimension the best acceptance probability equals 1−λmin(H)/r and a normalized witness attains it. This supplies the measurement and energy-gap argument; efficient synthesis and witness amplification are separate complexity claims.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"Lemma 6, Eqs. (11)–(13). Starting with row sparsity d and entry bound b>0, Hermiticity gives column sparsity. The constructed isometry and self-adjoint unitary swap compress to H/(db). The construction is in matrix frames.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"From a d-row-sparse Hermitian H with entry magnitudes at most b, one segmented QSP word simulates physical time τ/(db) for rational τ on every input with relative vector-norm error at most 2⁻ᵏ. Both counts belong to that word and charge its walk oracle and exact matrix gates. Theorem 3 (2017) counts elementary gates and position/entry queries with an optimal truncation schedule.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501","https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"Lemma 6, Eqs. (11)–(13), in frames. Row and column sparsity d and entry bound b yield isometries with overlap H/(db), related by a self-adjoint unitary swap. The declaration constructs the encoding mathematically.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-12-163"]},{"scope":"For an existing projected unitary encoding, a segmented, amplified QSP word approximates the time-evolution action on the embedded input. Its exact oracle and fixed-matrix gate counts use its own cutoff and segment parameters.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.118.010501"]},{"scope":"Appendix A, Theorem 1(3), with a uniform-clock phase-estimation variant. For a Hermitian A with all eigenvalue magnitudes in [d,1/4], a single run with clock M=max(2,⌈65536/(d⁶ε⁴)⌉) succeeds with probability at least d²/4. The postselected state is within ε of normalized A⁻¹b in the clean success registers. Its circuit uses 2(M−1) exact spectral-oracle queries and 4(M−1)+6 fixed-matrix gates.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"For signed gapped spectral data the uniform-clock reciprocal filter has Born success at least d²/4 and normalized pseudoinverse-state error at most 2η/d when M ≥ 4096/(d²η⁴) and η ≤ d/2. These are variant-specific precision bounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Counts the forward and inverse phase-estimation parts of the uniform-clock signed HHL variant. For a legal preparation and M≥2 the word is well formed and uses 2(M−1) oracle calls and 4(M−1)+6 fixed-matrix gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Eq. (1) of Subaşı, Somma and Orsucci and the HHL solution-state readout. For a unitary eigenbasis and nonzero real eigenvalues, the spectral Hermitian matrix’s pseudoinverse equals its ordinary inverse.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.10549","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.103.150502"]},{"scope":"Binomial recursion gives the two weighted nearest-neighbor terms in the action of the bare tridiagonal matrix. The formulas also cover the two end levels.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The positive vector with entries √(N choose j) has eigenvalue N/2 on the spin path. This supplies the norm-attaining vector for the crossed Hamiltonians.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The binomial spin amplitudes start at the zero endpoint and satisfy the constant-coefficient Schrödinger equation for the actual path matrix.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"A differentiable finite vector obeying v′ = Av evolves by the matrix exponential. The integrating factor exp((t−z)A) proves this auxiliary uniqueness statement.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Explicit all-time solution underlying Berry et al., Theorem 3. The level-j amplitude is √(N choose j) cos(t/2)^(N−j)(−i sin(t/2))^j. The far-end matrix entry is (−i sin(t/2))^N.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"At physical time π the spin path transfers the zero endpoint to the opposite endpoint with unit amplitude magnitude, as used in the proof of Berry et al., Theorem 3. The exact ket phase is (−i)^N.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The Fourier character of a reversible two-bit XOR row answer factors into the two phase queries for the crossing bits; missing end edges and inactive answer-mask positions contribute phase one.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Given the row vertex and the two incident edge bits, the decoder reconstructs every complex entry of that row, including endpoints.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The normalized two-bit Walsh transform is an involutive unitary. With coherent row addressing, conjugating the phase query by this fixed transform gives the computational-basis permutation that XORs both incident bits into the answer.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Input-independent unitary composition and closure of the row-query circuit under a final fixed gate preserve the query budget. The construction also handles the initial state.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"A conventional reversible XOR lookup compiles into one phase-basis row call. Each such call compiles into two input-bit phase queries. The amplitudes agree for all inputs and arbitrary fixed interleaving unitaries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The sign of the final crossed-path prefix parity equals the product of the N input-bit signs in the polynomial query lower bound.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"In the Berry parity construction, recovering the final track with bounded error needs at least N/4 full row queries. The endpoint version reads the Born probability of the physical evolution at π with additive error at most 1/4.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"The two tracks carry a strictly positive binomial eigenvector with eigenvalue N/2. The crossed entries are real and nonnegative, giving exact weighted row sums.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"At a coordinate maximizing |uᵢ|/wᵢ, the weighted row-sum identity bounds the absolute value of any eigenvalue of the crossed Hamiltonian by N/2.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"For every input string the physical Hilbert-space operator norm of the Hermitian two-track matrix is exactly N/2. At time π its scaled simulation time is πN/2.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"For a traceless finite Hermitian matrix, the absolute real part of any diagonal entry is at most half the sum of absolute eigenvalues. This bounds a basis-outcome probability error by the trace distance.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Tracing out finite simulator workspace gives a Hermitian output with trace equal to the squared norm of the global state. A diagonal entry sums the workspace probabilities for that output.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Physical evolution by the crossed Hamiltonian for time π places the initial endpoint exactly on the far-end parity track, with global phase (−i)^N. Its density matrix is the projector onto this endpoint.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Berry et al., Theorem 3. For every positive N the crossed row-computable Hamiltonians are 2-sparse with scaled time ‖H‖₂π = πN/2. A finite coherent whole-row query algorithm achieving trace distance ≤1/4 on all inputs after discarding any finite workspace uses at least N/4 queries. The reversible row answer contains both incident bits. Its address determines the neighboring levels and edge weights.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0150-x"]},{"scope":"Each sparse Ising bond is determined by an unordered pair of different sites. Three signed literal-copy bonds and three triangle bonds have distinct masks even when source variables or signed literals repeat.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"In the unrestricted two-body gadget each NAE clause gives exactly six occupied spin pairs; the Hamiltonian coefficients reproduce the signs in the executable edge table.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For the classical energy in Eq. (10), a signed two-site Walsh character contributes −1 exactly when the endpoint spins obey the sign on that bond. A negated literal may be handled by a spin flip.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Direct truth-table verification for a new signed Ising gadget. Three copy edges and an antiferromagnetic triangle have joint energy at least −4. Equality requires exact literal copies and a not-all-equal clause.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For m not-all-equal clauses, the unrestricted signed two-body glass energy is the sum of the explicit clause scores; the threshold −4m is attainable exactly for satisfiable formulas. This supplies a direct satisfiability source for the energy decision in Eqs. (10)–(11).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Each ordinary 3-CNF clause becomes two not-all-equal clauses sharing an auxiliary bit. A common anchor permits simultaneous spin inversion. Truth-table soundness and completeness give equivalence for empty formulas, repeated variables and negated literals.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For an ordinary 3-CNF on n variables with m clauses the unrestricted signed spin model has n+7m+1 sites and exactly 12m unit-strength bonds. Satisfiability is equivalent to attaining energy −8m.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"A single signed quantum thermal-energy estimate at β=13N and relative root-mean-square precision 1/(24N) decides ordinary 3-SAT with failure probability at most 1/4 on the linear-size unrestricted two-body gadget. Its sign-free counterpart at this query is estimated by −12m.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"A nearest-neighbour six-cycle with three negative bonds has minimum energy −4. At this energy the three terminals are not all equal; setting all three return spins to the third terminal realizes −4 for each legal terminal triple. A single copy bond has minimum −1.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"The sum of all nonnegative wire and cycle energy defects vanishes exactly when every wire is satisfied and every clause cycle is at its local minimum.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"At the compiled threshold each variable row and each private occurrence column carries a constant assignment. Its signed vertical contact makes the front terminal equal to the corresponding signed literal.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"A source assignment prescribes the base row spins, the occurrence-column spins and the three return spins of each clause. These formulas construct a lattice witness for every satisfying assignment.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"A new routing proof supplies the three-dimensional nearest-neighbour spin-glass decision premise of Troyer–Wiese Eq. (10). An NAE formula is satisfiable exactly when its three-layer copy rows, occurrence columns and frustrated six-cycles reach the compiled energy threshold.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Occurrence column numbers are unique. Copy rows, vertical contacts, column segments and the six rim slots at each clause occupy distinct positively oriented lattice positions.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Every generated endpoint belongs to the finite box of dimensions (3m+1) by (n+1) by 3. The existing cubicGlass forward-coordinate operation takes the unit axis step to that encoded endpoint.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Strictly positive axis steps have different endpoints and cannot reverse one another. Their unordered spin-flip masks uniquely identify the physical bond and satisfy the open-boundary cubic model’s self-edge test.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"The occupied masks of the existing cubicGlass representation equal the emitted edge masks, including their signs. Each compiled edge contributes exactly once to the occupied bond count.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Term-by-term agreement between finite cubicGlass energy and the routed lattice energy proves the bidirectional reduction from NAE satisfiability to the threshold for an actual three-layer nearest-neighbour Hamiltonian with couplings 0, ±1.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Sequential XOR spin flips realize a configuration path whose endpoint is the cumulative flip and whose unscaled series-expansion weight is the product of the corresponding signed model couplings, as in Eqs. (4)–(6).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"The mask on two distinct sites is their Boolean parity vector. Following six edges around a clause cycle flips each site twice and returns to the aligned configuration.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For every compiled NAE clause, its six-edge closed path has weight −1 in the signed quantum model and +1 in the comparison ferromagnet. This witnesses the actual sign problem on the constructed hardness instances.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Every source variable occurs in the finite name table. The executable first-match search returns its list index and counts at most the list length in equality comparisons. Array materialization preserves the literal table.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Dense occurrence-based renaming preserves satisfiability for arbitrary natural variable labels, including duplicates and unused numeric ranges. Constructing the table uses at most (3m)² equality comparisons on variable labels. This counts label comparisons; binary labels may span multiple bits.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"The executable sparse compiler enumerates all wire and rim edge identifiers, emits their signed nearest-neighbour positions and agrees with the cubic coupling table. Its output length is exactly the number of occupied bonds.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Counting the emitted sparse records gives both the classical threshold (minus the bond count plus twice the NAE clause count) and a deterministic sign-free thermal-energy estimate at β=13N with relative error ≤1/(24N). The emitted length is at most 3N.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For m source 3-CNF clauses, the three-layer box contains exactly 72m²+48m+6 spin sites. Substituting its size, β=13N and inverse precision 24N in Eq. (9) yields an explicit polynomial bound for the signed oracle call.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"End-to-end nearest-neighbour reduction from ordinary 3-SAT with arbitrary natural variable names. One integrable estimate having the stated relative mean-square bound decides satisfiability with error ≤1/4 using the emitted bond count as its threshold offset. At precisely that query the sign-free estimator is deterministic. Every nonempty instance also has a closed negative-weight path. An all-temperature sign-free sampling theorem and machine-level NP-to-BPP composition are outside this result.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (4), (8), (11). In the computational basis each two-site flip has a unique bond mask. Making all couplings nonnegative replaces every entry of −H and every spin-flip path weight by its exact absolute value.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (10). Walsh characters are signs. On a two-site mask the character is the product of the two classical Ising spins, so the absolute integer energy is bounded by the number of occupied bonds.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (11). The signed quantum Hamiltonian is exactly the sum of the two-site Pauli X flip matrices multiplied by −Jₑ, with couplings 0, ±1 in units of J.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (4) and Fig. 2. A four-site square with one negative bond has a concrete length-four closed spin-flip path of unscaled weight −1. The same bosonic path has weight +1.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (10)–(11). The Walsh basis gives an invertible change of basis from the matrix of signed two-site flips to the diagonal matrix of classical energies. This retains all eigenvalue multiplicities.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (3), (10)–(11). The quantum mean Tr[H exp(−βH)]/Tr[exp(−βH)] computed with the matrix exponential equals the Gibbs-weighted mean of the classical spin-glass energies for every real β.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Paragraph after Eq. (10). The finite energy minimum exists. Shifting to one ground configuration keeps the Gibbs mean, yields a partition sum at least one, and bounds all integer excitation energies by twice the bond count.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Paragraph after Eq. (10). For N spins with integer excitation energies in [0,D], D>0 and β≥0, the finite-temperature energy exceeds the minimum by less than 2ᴺ D exp(−β).","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Paragraph after Eq. (10), energy unit J=1. For N>0, at most 3N occupied bonds and β≥N log 2+log(12N), a ground energy ≤t implies thermal mean \u003ct+1/2. Otherwise the thermal mean is ≥t+1. The non-strict NO endpoint includes flat spectra and gives the required decision gap. For physical coupling J>0, the proof scales the temperature as βJ and the energy window as J/2.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Paragraph after Eq. (10). An inverse temperature β=13N in units J=1 satisfies the published logarithmic lower bound for every positive spin count.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (9) and the reduction after Eq. (10). The absolute thermal energy is bounded by the number of occupied bonds, converting an inverse-linear relative error target into constant absolute error.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (9) and note [9]. A single signed-energy estimate at β=13N with relative root mean squared error ≤1/(24N) decides the integer threshold with failure probability ≤1/4 over any probability space. This implication concerns the spin-glass decision problem; an NP-class reduction is additional.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"After Eq. (11). All-aligned spins attain energy −m for m nonzero ferromagnetic bonds, and this is a ground configuration.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Sign-free comparison after Eq. (11), restricted to the thermal decision query. At β=13N, returning the negative number of occupied bonds deterministically estimates the bosonic mean to relative error ≤1/(24N). The zero-bond case is exact. No mixing-time hypothesis is needed for this query.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (10). In an open three-dimensional box of size a×b×c there are at most three positive-direction nearest-neighbour bonds per vertex, with boundary self-edges excluded.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (8)–(11), note [9]. For an explicit three-dimensional lattice with couplings 0, ±1, one signed thermal estimate gives a bounded-error ground-threshold decision. At exactly its temperature and tolerance the bosonic comparison is computed by the occupied-bond count. Barahona’s NP-hardness result, uniform machine complexity and all-temperature cluster mixing are separate claims.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eq. (4). For each integer k≥0 the closed paths with k+1 transitions sum to the signed trace Taylor coefficient β^(k+1) Tr[(−H)^(k+1)]/(k+1)!. Including the trace of the identity at order zero, the complete series converges to Tr[exp(−βH)].","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Eqs. (6)–(8). At nonnegative β the ferromagnetic Taylor weights are exactly the absolute signed weights. At any order of nonzero signed mass the signed observable mean is recovered by the ratio of two bosonic expectations.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"Product differentiation and a norm estimate for a bounded observable conjugated by the supplied contractive evolution.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Differentiating an idempotent yields the double-commutator identity for its tangent.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1143/jpsj.5.435"]},{"scope":"The commutator-corrected evolution exactly intertwines a differentiable idempotent on [0,1]. A commuting generator and a contractive propagator with specified derivatives and inverse are hypotheses.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1143/jpsj.5.435"]},{"scope":"For a supplied slow propagator and a bounded differentiable solution of the exact commutator equation, the intertwining defect is at most (2B+sD)/T. Avron–Elgart’s general gapless theorem allows an approximate commutator and gives convergence without a universal rate.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"Leakage is at most (4L/g+sD)/T when a reduced inverse of the shifted generator and a differentiable commutator correction obey the stated uniform bounds.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1143/jpsj.5.435","https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"A two-band generator a(s)I+gap·P(s) with fixed nonzero splitting, contractive idempotents, bounded first and second projection derivatives and supplied slow evolutions has O(1/T) leakage. The limit is at s=1.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1143/jpsj.5.435","https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050620"]},{"scope":"For finite classical configurations with unit reward per occupied vertex and uniform edge penalty greater than the vertex count, the energy minimizers are exactly the maximum independent sets. Physical geometry and preparation time are outside this energy model.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.10816"]},{"scope":"In the plane of unit good and bad components, the projected amplitude is sin θ, the state has unit norm and the inner product is the cosine of the angle difference.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"The good-subspace sign reflection negates the angle in the two-component representation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"The two reflections rotate by 2θ. Starting at angle θ, k ideal iterations give angle (2k+1)θ and good-outcome probability sin²((2k+1)θ), as in the amplitude-amplification analysis.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"For known a=sin²θ with 0\u003cθ≤π/2, choosing the integer iteration count gives success at least 1−a using at most π/(4√a) marking reflections. State preparation and its inverse are separate operations.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/305/05215"]},{"scope":"Each complex SU(2) block preserves the pair norm and can split a nonnegative branch radius into its two specified amplitudes, including a zero branch.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0407010"]},{"scope":"Induction on prefixes proves normalization of all outputs of the specified recursive circuit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0407010"]},{"scope":"The binary prefix tree has 2ⁿ−1 controlled pair rotations and a separate root phase. This counts blocks before elementary-gate decomposition.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0407010"]},{"scope":"Every normalized complex n-qubit amplitude table is obtained exactly by a recursive prefix-controlled rotation circuit including a global phase. Costs are measured in prefix-controlled blocks.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0407010"]},{"scope":"An orthonormal frame extends to an orbital unitary. Its exterior power sends the selected occupation determinant to the target wedge.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036"]},{"scope":"The coefficient of a fixed occupation determinant in a wedge of orbitals is the corresponding orbital-matrix minor.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036"]},{"scope":"A mode fixed by orbital gates is unchanged by their product, and its coordinate is unchanged under inverse action.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036"]},{"scope":"A two-mode orbital unitary can eliminate one coefficient. Iterating eliminations prepares a normalized vector on its allowed support.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036"]},{"scope":"Unrestricted pairs of orbital modes suffice to factor an orbital unitary. Exterior-power lifting respects the product of gates.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.120.110501"]},{"scope":"Any target orthonormal frame gives a Slater determinant preparable by a finite list of number-preserving one- or two-mode gates. The circuit model permits unrestricted mode pairs and asserts existence of a gate list.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevapplied.9.044036","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.120.110501"]},{"scope":"For repulsive step coupling x≥0 a real nonnegative Hirsch parameter solves cosh γ=exp(x/2).","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059"]},{"scope":"Hirsch’s discrete spin-field identity for the shifted Hubbard interaction is checked on all four local occupation pairs under cosh γ=exp(x/2).","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059"]},{"scope":"Each field weight separates into spin-up and spin-down factors, and independent binary fields decouple the product over finitely many sites.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059"]},{"scope":"Distributivity expands a power of a finite noncommutative sum into all ordered field histories.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(86)90107-7"]},{"scope":"A fixed number of split kinetic/interaction steps equals the exact sum of all discrete Hubbard field histories.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059","https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(86)90107-7"]},{"scope":"For a finite field decomposition by weighted one-body exterior maps, a Slater state propagates as the exact signed or complex weighted sum of propagated orbital wedges.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(86)90107-7"]},{"scope":"A trial exterior-product bra evaluated on the finite field-history sum gives a weighted sum of orbital overlap determinants.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(86)90107-7"]},{"scope":"With nonzero individual and total overlaps, importance-weighted local energies reproduce the mixed estimator. A left eigenbra gives its eigenvalue on each overlapping walker.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.90.136401"]},{"scope":"For the paper’s max(0,cos Δθ) weight projection, loss in the product of cosine factors is bounded by half the sum of squared phase increments. The argument fixes every other weight factor.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.90.136401"]},{"scope":"For a positive guide and positive propagated guide on a finite state space, the guided transition and branching factors expand Gᵏ exactly. Nonnegative entries of G make the transition a Markov kernel.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.465195"]},{"scope":"A nowhere-zero guide has constant branching exactly when it is an eigenvector. For a shifted Euler propagator, the branching factor is one minus the step times the local-energy offset.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.465195"]},{"scope":"With a symmetric nonnegative finite propagator and a positive eigenvector guide, the guided kernel obeys detailed balance and preserves the squared guide weights.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.465195"]},{"scope":"For a real symmetric lattice model and a nowhere-zero guide, the fixed-node quadratic form adds a sum of nonnegative penalties on sign-flip edges.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.51.13039"]},{"scope":"The finite real lattice fixed-node ground-energy infimum bounds the original one from above for any guide nonzero on every site, by positivity of the sign-flip correction.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.51.13039"]},{"scope":"Trial energies agree exactly when the trial-to-guide ratios match on each sign-flip edge. If a normalized minimizing state matches those ratios, the two ground energies coincide, including for an exact nonvanishing guide.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.51.13039"]},{"scope":"Randomized integer rounding has nonnegative normalized masses and the intended first moment. The two independent roundings implement spawning and diagonal death or cloning.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3193710"]},{"scope":"A normalized proposal and normalized rounding laws define finite one-walker and independent-population outcome laws. Summing over other walkers recovers the selected marginal.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3193710"]},{"scope":"Local cancellation leaves at most one sign at each determinant and preserves the net signed walker count exactly.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3193710"]},{"scope":"For a supported spawning proposal and a fixed shift, the expected one-step signed counts give the Euler imaginary-time CI update, for one walker and for a finite population. This is a first-moment identity.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3193710"]},{"scope":"The characteristic function of a standard real Gaussian gives the scalar phase-average identity for real v and τ≥0.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.3.77"]},{"scope":"For a finite Hermitian channel V and τ≥0, the Gaussian average of exp(i√τ xV) exists and equals exp(−τV²/2).","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.3.77"]},{"scope":"In a finite eigenbasis with nonzero initial ground overlap, normalization gives explicit Born weights. The infidelity is the excited mass divided by total filtered mass.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.431514","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-019-0704-4"]},{"scope":"A positive lower bound on excitation energies exponentially suppresses the unnormalized excited mass, and the resulting exponential envelope tends to zero.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.431514","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-019-0704-4"]},{"scope":"For nonnegative imaginary time, initial ground mass greater than zero and a finite gapped spectrum, infidelity is bounded by the initial excited-to-ground mass ratio times exp(−2Δτ), so ground-sector fidelity tends to one. Evolution here uses the exact diagonal filter.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.431514","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-019-0704-4"]},{"scope":"The normalized energy excess lies between zero and the spectral width times the infidelity envelope. Finiteness of the spectrum gives an energy excess tending to zero.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.431514","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-019-0704-4"]},{"scope":"The shifted lattice interaction step is its diagonal exponential. For β,U≥0 the exact sum over binary field histories realizes the m-step Lie product at time β.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059","https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9939-1959-0108732-6"]},{"scope":"At fixed β≥0 and repulsive U, the finite-dimensional, exactly summed field-history propagator tends to exp(−β(K+V)), including after application to a fixed state. The limit refines the time grid.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.28.4059","https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9939-1959-0108732-6"]},{"scope":"The normalized finite Fourier kernel is one at the target, vanishes on other exact grid phases, and has magnitude ≤2/(Nδ) for a unit phase at chord distance at least δ from the target.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"Normalizing the postselected Fourier-filter branch gives the kernel-weighted amplitudes. A positive initial ground overlap keeps the normalizer positive.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"The conditional ground-sector weight is initial ground mass divided by branch weight. A normalized input makes the heralding probability positive and at most one.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"If every excited phase is an Nth root of unity distinct from the target, the ideal target readout eliminates all excited amplitudes. The success weight is the initial ground mass.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"With positive initial ground mass and all excited phases separated by chord distance δ, the finite N-point filter bounds the heralding probability and gives an explicit conditional-fidelity lower bound.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.83.5162"]},{"scope":"For finite real signed weights the absolute-weight average of the sign gives the mean sign. Dividing the signed numerator estimate by it recovers the reweighted average when the signed partition sum is nonzero. Lean division is total.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For independent product weights the mean sign factorizes. The explicit two-outcome weights 3 and −1 give mean sign 2⁻ᴸ.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For n positive, pairwise-independent sign draws from the absolute-weight law give variance (1−s²)/n. For the explicit product example a relative RMS target δ requires nδ²≥4ᴸ−1. The paper’s separate NP-hardness reduction concerns a generic solution of the sign problem.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.170201"]},{"scope":"For any supplied diagonal filter with constant nonzero ground gain and relative squared excited gain ≤q\u003c1, repeated application contracts the excited-to-ground odds by qᵏ. The minimax polynomial construction and query cost in Lin–Tong are additional results.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2020-11-11-361"]},{"scope":"In a finite matrix space e^(−T)He^T has the reference as an eigenvector precisely when H has e^T times the reference as an eigenvector, at the same energy. The result addresses exact similarity before any truncation or amplitude solver.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1727484"]},{"scope":"For finitely many couplings and strictly positive diagonal gaps, the sum of negative squared couplings divided by their gaps is nonpositive. CASPT2 in the cited work also treats nondiagonal blocks.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.462209"]},{"scope":"A simple target level gives nonzero energy denominators. Hermiticity turns the contraction of the first-order response into the real quadratic shift.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19263851302"]},{"scope":"In a finite unperturbed eigenbasis with simple target and Hermitian perturbation, the coefficient equations through degree two admit a solution, and fix the first-order state and first two energy corrections in intermediate normalization.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19263851302"]},{"scope":"The second-order truncated state and energy from coefficient matching obey an exact polynomial residual identity with factor t³ for the finite matrix family. This controls the quasimode residual.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19263851302"]},{"scope":"A bounded Hamiltonian and nested spans of determinants give nonincreasing Ritz energy whenever the smaller span contains a nonzero trial determinant.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1950.0036","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"The Ritz infimum in a sampled span is exact if the span contains a nonzero vector attaining the full-space infimum. Sampling that span with adequate probability is an additional algorithmic question.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adu9991","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Dimension counting gives a nonzero vector with prescribed spectral support. The energy quadratic form on such a vector obeys the minimum and maximum eigenvalue bounds on that support.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.43.830"]},{"scope":"For an m-dimensional compression of an n-dimensional Hermitian operator, each compressed eigenvalue lies between the two full eigenvalues with the Poincaré index shifts (Lean orders them decreasingly).","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.43.830"]},{"scope":"The nested spans of finite prefixes of an arbitrary nonzero starting vector’s orbit give nonincreasing Ritz infima.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Enlarging the Hamiltonian-power Krylov space of a nonzero vector by one step cannot raise its Ritz infimum.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.045.026","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Enlarging the orbit space of a nonzero vector under any unitary step cannot raise the Hamiltonian Ritz infimum. The statement assumes exact subspaces and overlaps.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.9b01125","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"For a bounded operator and an orthogonally projectable subspace, a nonzero trial vector solves the compressed eigenproblem exactly when its full residual is orthogonal to the trial subspace.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://www.unige.ch/~gander/HistoricalReferences/GALERKIN.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Scalar polynomial and quadratic-form variation identities for the real form-domain pairing, external coupling and symmetric internal energy used in the weak eigen-equation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"A minimizer of the real-form Rayleigh quotient satisfies the weak eigen-equation against every vector of the assumed form domain.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"Two minimizing wavefunctions with matching squared norms and densities share a ground state for the two potentials, by the variational inequalities.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"In the real-form setting, equal densities of equal-norm ground states give potentials differing by a constant under test-function separation, rigidity of the one-body lift and almost-everywhere nonvanishing. The common-state lemmas start with a shared ground state and derive constant lift, then constant potential.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"The normalized-trial energy infimum and the potential-dual supremum give the inequalities used to establish the density variational identity.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/qua.560240302"]},{"scope":"For normalized representable densities in the supplied form setting, the potential-dual supremum defines a universal extended-real functional and recovers the ground-energy infimum. The identity uses infima.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/qua.560240302"]},{"scope":"At the density of an attained normalized ground state the supremum-defined functional equals that state’s internal energy.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1002/qua.560240302"]},{"scope":"The inverse-distance kernel is locally integrable in three dimensions. Its coordinate lifts give finite nuclear-attraction integrals against marginals of real smooth compactly supported molecular trial states.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062","https://doi.org/10.1002/qua.560240302"]},{"scope":"For finitely many fixed point nuclei in three dimensions, the extended-real energy infimum over real, smooth, compactly supported, normalized antisymmetric trial functions equals the density constrained-search infimum. The proof discharges the nuclear-attraction integrability condition within this trial class.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062"]},{"scope":"A normalized molecular trial gives a unit Born measure and a one-body density measure of mass N. Integrating a potential against that density sums its electron-coordinate expectations under the marginal integrability hypothesis.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062"]},{"scope":"The energy infimum over normalized smooth compactly supported real antisymmetric trial states on (ℝ³)ᴺ equals the infimum over densities of the internal-energy fiber infimum plus the potential pairing. This module assumes marginal integrability, including for fixed nuclei.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.12.6062"]},{"scope":"For one or more three-dimensional coordinates, almost-everywhere constancy of the sum of copies of a one-body potential implies its almost-everywhere constancy.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"For two finite point-nucleus potentials in the supplied molecular form setting, ground states with equal density and equal squared norm determine the potential difference up to an almost-everywhere constant, assuming separation and the almost-everywhere nonvanishing of one state.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.136.b864"]},{"scope":"The energy-weighted bound sums termwise inequalities against a normalized family of weights.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.43.830"]},{"scope":"In normalized eigenbasis coefficients, vanishing on all eigenstates below the target energy implies that the mean energy is at least the target.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.43.830"]},{"scope":"With an exact common eigenbasis and diagonal penalties that lift every energy to at least the target while leaving the target unchanged, the minimum penalized expectation equals the target energy. Degeneracies are allowed.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2019-07-01-156"]},{"scope":"A nonzero finite-basis Slater determinant is antisymmetric, so its Rayleigh quotient bounds the infimum over the fermion sector. The proof concerns the trial-state upper bound used in Hartree–Fock.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01340294","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"A weighted sum of determinants built from row-equivariant orbital matrices is antisymmetric under particle permutations. This proves the symmetry property of the ansatz under the stated equivariance.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevresearch.2.033429"]},{"scope":"In a finite orthonormal eigenbasis, the Hermitian expectation is the sum of energies weighted by squared overlaps.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-9565/2/1/015004"]},{"scope":"The lower bound in Hangleiter et al., Lemma 2, is proved for normalized pure states and extended to a possibly degenerate ground sector. A gap Δ and energy error ε certify ground-sector weight ≥1−ε/Δ.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-9565/2/1/015004","https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms1147"]},{"scope":"Boundedness of a continuous operator bounds its real Rayleigh quotients below. The full-space and restricted infima therefore bound each admissible nonzero trial quotient.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"For a self-adjoint operator on a nontrivial finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space, the Rayleigh-quotient infimum is an eigenvalue and bounds every nonzero trial quotient.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Enlarging a nonzero trial subspace decreases its Ritz infimum, which still bounds the full-space infimum from above.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Contracting fixed left and right environments with a free site tensor gives the entries of the explicit linear map to physical states.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.69.2863","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.72.180403"]},{"scope":"The compressed Hamiltonian B†HB is Hermitian for Hermitian H. The local quotient using overlap B†B agrees with the physical Rayleigh quotient on the embedded tensor.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.69.2863","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.72.180403"]},{"scope":"With fixed environments an exact minimizing site update cannot raise the energy. For an isometric embedding of a nonzero finite-dimensional site space and Hermitian H, a lowest effective eigenvector attains the local minimum. This concerns a single update.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.69.2863","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevb.72.180403"]},{"scope":"For a fixed bipartition, a bond of size χ bounds the coefficient matrix rank. Rank at most D is equivalent to a factorization across a D-dimensional bond. Vidal’s circuit simulation additionally requires tracking ranks and updating tensors throughout the computation.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"A unitary ansatz acting on a normalized reference gives a variational upper bound. The parameterized energies attain the variational ground value if the ansatz reaches a ground eigenvector.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"Squared amplitudes in the finite orthonormal sampling basis sum to one for a unit trial vector.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.138.a442"]},{"scope":"The finite-basis Born average of local energy equals the trial expectation, including at zero amplitudes via zero weight. For a normalized Hermitian trial problem its real part bounds the lowest eigenvalue. There are separate real-matrix and complex Hilbert-space formulations.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.138.a442","https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1909.135.1"]},{"scope":"The squared residual at a real reference energy equals the Born-weighted centered second moment plus omitted-node contributions. Eigenstates have zero moment. The converse requires Hψ to vanish at every node of ψ.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/0310035"]},{"scope":"The finite Metropolis acceptance flow is the minimum of the two weights after removing the symmetric proposal. Adding the rejection probability gives unit row sums and, for a stochastic proposal, nonnegative entries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1699114"]},{"scope":"With a symmetric stochastic proposal, the finite Metropolis chain preserves the Born distribution of a normalized trial state by detailed balance. This establishes stationarity. Ergodicity needs additional hypotheses.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1699114","https://doi.org/10.1103/physrev.138.a442"]},{"scope":"For a finite real symmetric Hamiltonian and differentiable real trial amplitudes with full support and nonzero mass, the derivative of the normalized energy is twice the covariance of local energy and the logarithmic derivative.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0502553"]},{"scope":"A counterexample to an inference about uniform complexity. The abstract hierarchy has work L^k and inverse error 2^k. It escapes every C L^a (1/ε)^b bound along L = 1/ε. It does not construct a molecular coupled-cluster counterexample.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37587-6"]},{"scope":"A schedule using only the modulus gives at least 2/t success per run of the chosen ForShor circuit, using t = 64(1+floor(log₂(1+floor(log₂N)))). The bound comes from its matrix recovery theorem with κ=1/π².","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The allocated compiled circuit measures a clock of size 2^floor(log₂(2N²)). This specifies the physical input to the decoder; the ideal paper-clock theorem separately uses N²≤Q\u003c2N².","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The known-N schedule has failure at most exp(−2) \u003c 1/3 under the actual product of independent compiled-circuit Born laws. Those tuple weights sum to one.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Every decoded denominator fits the clock. Streaming the records through bit-level decoding, a zero check, and a minimum selector agrees exactly with the reference minimum of the nonzero verified estimates, with all those data-path operations counted.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"The physical bounded-error statement uses the executable Boolean decoder and streaming minimum on fresh zero-state preparations of the same compiled order-finding circuit. The number of preparations is determined from the known modulus.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Across the complete known-modulus repetition schedule for a valid unit input, the allocated circuit uses O(L⁴) logical gate operations and the decoder plus aggregation use ≤40,000,000 L⁵ Boolean data-path operations at sufficiently large input width L. These are loose upper bounds in the stated cost models.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"Shor §§3–5. The normalized ideal paper-clock experiment has one-shot success ≥(4/π²)φ(r)/r and constant error after O(log log r) independent shots. A concrete compiled circuit, run for a count computed solely from N, yields the exact order with error \u003c1/3 after Boolean decoding and aggregation. Total logical gates and classical data-path operations are polynomial in the bit length of N, with the distinct ideal and compiled clocks explicit. The separate uniformity requirement in §2 concerns the classical time to generate gate descriptions. Its cost is outside this execution bound.","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1137/s0097539795293172","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20342"]},{"scope":"For neighboring wires the two-qubit SWAP matrix maps the dense amplitude to the amplitude with the input bits exchanged. Applying it twice restores the state.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"An addressed invertible two-qubit gate retains its left inverse; inserting SWAPs before and after it preserves invertibility throughout the circuit.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Routing bookkeeping identifies both physical target bits and the basis input after replacing only those bits. Adding a spectator preserves its value throughout the route.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Routing inserts one outward and one return SWAP per skipped site and one central gate. A single arbitrary-pair operation compiles to at most 2n adjacent operations on n qubits.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Vidal’s nonconsecutive-gate routing step before Theorem 1. The dense action of the compiled SWAP sequence is exactly the intended matrix action on the selected original pair.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Every pair of numerical positions i \u003c j \u003c n occurs among the simulator’s addresses, including the two ends of the chain.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Coordinates of computational-basis strings agree with the explicit wire permutation of a nearest-neighbor SWAP. The indexing permutation is its own inverse.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Vidal Eq. (2) bounds Schmidt rank over every bipartition. Prefix/suffix coefficient matrices over every permutation express this bound, so each SWAP inherits it on every cut.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"All intermediate states introduced by routing are wire permutations of the pre- or post-gate logical state. A physical rank bound on the original circuit suffices throughout the adjacent simulation.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"An entire program on arbitrary qubit pairs compiles to an exactly equivalent adjacent circuit of invertible gates of length at most 2nG for G original operations.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"A one-wire operation acts with an identity on its unused neighbor. Encoding mixed circuits this way preserves dense evolution, invertibility and the rank promises at the original gate boundaries.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"From a nonzero product input and physical all-bipartition rank bound χ, the actual routed simulation on n ≥ 2 wires gives the dense final product expectation. Work including readout is ≤ 2nG(24χ³ + 40χ²) + n(20χ³ + 8χ²); peak scalar storage is ≤ 2nχ² + 36χ² + 4χ.","relation":"derived","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Singleton-register base case: arbitrary sequences reduce to ordinary matrix–vector products in two amplitudes, with eight scalar operations per gate.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"Theorem 1, all widths in the scalar-arithmetic model. Given nonzero product input and G arbitrary one- or two-qubit invertible gates, χ bounds the physical Schmidt ranks across all bipartitions at the original steps. An explicit classical algorithm returns the exact product-observable expectation in ≤ 200(G+1)(n+1)(χ+1)³ operations and ≤ 100(n+1)(χ+1)² scalar slots. This includes local Born projectors on normalized states and all unitary circuits. The paper’s separate finite-precision discussion is in note [10].","relation":"paper-result","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.91.147902"]},{"scope":"The joint three-copy majority effect of binary witness measurements is monotone in the individual effects. Equal scalar effects give the cubic 3q²−2q³.","relation":"supporting","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]},{"scope":"The three-copy case of witness amplification (Lemma 14.1) gives acceptance at most 3q²−2q³ for every normalized, possibly entangled witness when the one-copy effect is bounded by qI.","relation":"specialization","papers":["https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/047"]}],"relations":{"paper-result":"Paper result","specialization":"Special case","derived":"Derived result","supporting":"Supporting lemma","source-note":"Source cited in Lean · comparison pending"},"byNode":{"AaronsonAmbainisPort.Main/AaronsonAmbainis.aaronson_ambainis":39,"AaronsonAmbainisPort.Main/AaronsonAmbainis.aaronson_ambainis_explicit":40,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.acceptance_influential":41,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.antibondingInfluence":44,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.antibondingVariance":44,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.antibonding_influential":45,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.mean_oneBit":43,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainis/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.orbitalPopulation_influential":42,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.antibondingPopulation":54,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.bondingNormalized":53,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.bondingReadout_unitary":53,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.halfAmplitude_sq":53,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.onsiteHamiltonian_isHermitian":51,"FQChem.Complexity.AaronsonAmbainisOrbitals/FQChem.Complexity.PhaseQuery.onsite_e